<div class="ct">★★ READ THIS BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE &mdash; how the open position ended</div>
<p><strong>This report was written for the week ending 2026-08-21 and is being published on
2026-08-26, so the biggest single item in it has already resolved and you need the ending before
you read the rest.</strong> The day rider was long 4 MNQU6 from 29,491.625,
opened 2026-08-21T13:13:06Z, and it went through the CME halt into the weekend unmanaged.
<strong>You closed it by hand on Monday 2026-08-24 at 11:59:39Z, at
29,224.00, for &minus;$2,155.44 net.</strong> That single position is larger than everything
else in this report put together, and it is not in any of the totals below &mdash; it closed on
the 24th, and this report's books stop on the 21st.</p>
<p><strong>&#9733; Not one of the automatic paths closed it, and the reason is two separate faults
in a row.</strong> The IB gateway entered a partial wedge at about 19:57Z on the Friday, and the
rider's own hard-flat, the watchdog, the EOD flatten and the reconciler all share one
<code>ib.connectAsync</code>, so all four died on the same call. The EOD flatten's timer is
<strong>Mon&ndash;Fri</strong>, so a Friday failure gets no weekend catch-up at all.
<code>gazbot7-weekend-flatten.timer</code> &mdash; built for exactly this &mdash; then fired
<strong>119 times</strong> between 22:00Z and 23:59Z on the Sunday. <strong>Every one failed:
105 of them on the wedge, and then, once the 23:45Z gateway close had healed the wedge, the last
14 on a <code>PermissionError</code> writing root-owned <code>data/day_rider.env</code> &mdash;
which happens BEFORE the claim write and BEFORE the page.</strong> So it never placed an order and
never told anyone. Fixing either fault alone still gets you nothing: the file-mode fix buys 14
attempts that would all have hit the wedge, and the gateway fix buys 105 that would all have hit
the file mode.</p>
<p>&#9733;&nbsp;<strong>AND THE TIMER IS STILL ARMED.</strong> It is written to self-disable once
the venue reads flat, but it dies before it ever gets that far, so it never disabled itself:
<code>systemctl list-timers</code> has it next elapsing <strong>Sunday 2026-08-30 22:00Z</strong>,
where it will re-arm the rider and write a bare-stamp claim &mdash; which means <em>claim
everything</em> &mdash; against whatever happens to be open that night. It has to be deleted, and
that is SATURDAY&nbsp;#1.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="honest"><span class="n">★</span> THE HONEST HEADLINE &mdash; a small red week, and the
loss is not where you would look for it</h2>

<p class="lead">The desk booked <strong>&minus;$247.50</strong> across 43 clean legs
(49 contracts &mdash; the day rider's legs are 1&ndash;3 lots each, so a row count is
not a lot count),
<strong>3 of the five days green</strong>. Three sessions made $1,120.50
between them and two lost &minus;$1,368.00, and essentially
all of that loss is <strong>one day-rider position on Thursday</strong> that ran for six hours with
no stop in the water. The tournament &mdash; the eight base gates filling six live slots that most
of this report is about &mdash; traded only 36 legs all week for &minus;$58.50, because four of its
six slots were stood down and never fired at all.</p>

<p class="lead"><strong>The three things worth carrying into Saturday.</strong>
<em>One:</em> the standdown was right. Firing all six gates mechanically through this week's own
tape loses <strong>&minus;$1,905.36</strong> on 117 fires (Movement&nbsp;2), so the four silent
slots are the best-performing thing on the desk.
<em>Two:</em> your hands made $550.00 on 16 claims and the machine's own profit exits made
almost nothing &mdash; but the study that tried to automate your hand comes back
<strong>&minus;$4 out-of-sample</strong>, so the answer is not to copy it.
<em>Three:</em> the week's real damage was done by things that are not strategies at all &mdash; a
session-roll bug, an off-tape fill, a missing stop and a wedged gateway. &#9733; <strong>And the
sharpest version of that last one:</strong> the four lots are a <em>missing-stop</em> incident that a
wedge then made unfixable, not a wedge incident. Every stop width tested &mdash; all seven, from
0.5&times; to 3&times;ATR &mdash; closes that position within nineteen minutes of entry, six and a
half hours before the gateway stopped answering. It eventually cost &minus;$2,155.44, which is more than
the rest of the week's book combined; the stop would have cost a fraction of it.</p>

