<h2 id="evidence"><span class="n">§</span> The evidence behind those plays</h2><p class="lead">Everything from here down is the working. Two halves, one document. It opens with <strong>Part 0</strong>, which answers the only question that survives past Friday &mdash; <em>are we getting better?</em> &mdash; day by day, green or red, straight off the books. <strong>Then the warm half</strong>: what the live desk actually did (Part 1, with <strong>the run charts</strong> inside it &mdash; every fill of the week marked on its own session&rsquo;s price path, which is the fastest way to see what the prose is describing). Then the section that matters most &mdash; <strong>Rehabilitation</strong> (Part 1.5), where every red thing this week is walked through the full treatment instead of benched at face value, and where the failed treatments are printed by name because the failures are the point. Then <strong>the day rider</strong> (Part 1.6), which this week is not a footnote to the tournament but the bigger of the two books. Then the shadow board and the promotion battery (Part 2), the mid-week leads re-run adversarially including the whole gold programme (Part 2.5), and the <strong>Router Operation Review</strong> (Part 2.6). <strong>Then the cold, tape-first half</strong>: we ignore everything the desk did and ask, from the raw tape and order book, <strong>where was the money and did we show up?</strong> &mdash; on MNQ <em>and</em> on gold (M1) &mdash; then test whether the gates we already own could have caught the runs we missed (M2), and finally build gates from scratch and backtest them to death, the ones that failed included, that being the point (M3). Plain English, honest money, every claim a table &mdash; and every reprice a ceiling.</p><p class="lead">&#9733; <strong>Where work did not run this cycle, it says so in red at the point where it should have been</strong>, naming the phase and what you are not getting. A fragment that is missing or stale is replaced by a dated red placeholder automatically; a phase that ran <em>partially</em>, or on inputs that have since moved, gets a red banner at the top of its section. <strong>There are two of those this week &mdash; Part&nbsp;1.5, whose rehab phase timed out, and Movement&nbsp;2, which was computed against a census that has since been re-frozen.</strong> Nothing is silently omitted, and a gap is never a null result: &ldquo;we looked and found nothing&rdquo; and &ldquo;nobody looked&rdquo; are opposite instructions for next week. <em>(&#9733; Last week a banner on this page said four of Movement&nbsp;3's hunts never ran. They ran &mdash; on the Monday, after the section had been frozen &mdash; and Movement&nbsp;3 has been rebuilt from all seven dossiers, which are reproduced in it in full. A stale honesty banner is worse than none, because it spends the reader's trust on a falsehood.)</em></p><div class="v7toc" id="contents"><h3>What is in here</h3><ol><li><span class="lab">Front</span> &mdash; <a href="#honest">the honest headline</a>, <a href="#scorecard">the scorecard</a>, <a href="#actions">WHAT TO ACTUALLY DO</a></li><li><span class="lab">Part 0</span> &mdash; <a href="#sec0">Are we getting better? — every day the desk has traded, green or red</a></li><li><span class="lab">Part 1</span> &mdash; <a href="#sec1">The live desk — what the paper tournament and the day rider actually did</a></li><li><span class="lab">Part 1.5</span> &mdash; <a href="#sec2">Live-desk REHABILITATION — never bench a gate at face value</a></li><li><span class="lab">Part 1.6</span> &mdash; <a href="#sec2b">THE DAY RIDER — the second desk, its week, and the four lots it left open</a></li><li><span class="lab">Part 2</span> &mdash; <a href="#sec3">The shadow desk, the promotion battery, and the ruler that reordered the board</a></li><li><span class="lab">Part 2.5</span> &mdash; <a href="#sec4">Mid-week musings — every lead from the desk chat, tested hard</a></li><li><span class="lab">Part 2.6</span>