<p class="ln"><strong>What is NOT in dispute:</strong> having a stop at all. Every width beats what happened on Thursday, <strong>This is not hindsight about direction</strong> &mdash; it is not asking the rider
to have known the tape was going down. It is asking it to have had any exit at all, at any of six
widths, all of which are cheaper than holding to your hand at 19:33. The rider ran naked for
6.7 hours.</p>

<div class="rev3"><div class="ct">★ AND A LOSING RIDER IS STRUCTURALLY SILENT — that is the real defect</div><p>The reason this ran for six hours is that nothing was shouting. The rider pages on peak give-back and on stalls; below its arm threshold there is no stop, no trail and no alert, so a position that is simply losing produces no output at all. Both of the tools you would naturally check print &ldquo;flat, $0&rdquo; while it happens, because the trade has not CLOSED yet and they count closed rows.</p><p><strong>The rehabilitation of the rider is not an entry change and it is not a threshold change. It is a stop.</strong> A <strong>3.0&times;ATR</strong> protective stop on the rider's own book, sized off its entry ATR, is the single highest-value change on this week's card &mdash; and &#9733; REV2 corrects both halves of what Rev1 said here: not 1.5&times;, and not &ldquo;$1,000 on one day&rdquo; but +$468 on the week&rsquo;s closed book. It is also the change that removes the report&rsquo;s headline: every width tested, including 3.0&times;, closes Friday&rsquo;s 13:13Z entry by 13:31:55Z &mdash; six and a half hours before the gateway wedged.</p></div>

