l (11.68 vs 11.69, 22.62 vs 22.64, 11.0% vs 11.0%). Neither is wrong and they never disagreed about the tape. The lake and capture.db <em>agree</em> at ~10&ndash;11% once put on the same basis, so the &ldquo;two independent measurements&rdquo; framing was never the issue either. <strong>Which one sizes a ladder: the SESSION one</strong>, because a floor can only bind in hours the gate can fire, and the desk refuses new entries 00:00&ndash;07:00Z anyway.</p><p class="ln"> <strong>&#9733; REV2: &ldquo;the floor sits at the ninetieth percentile&rdquo; is true of the ALL-HOURS denominator and false of the session one, where it is the p75</strong> &mdash; and on this week&rsquo;s session tape it was reachable in 29.5% of minutes. So it is not a p90 off-switch; it is a p75 filter that <em>behaves</em> like an off-switch, and the reason is not the height of the floor. It is that grind&rsquo;s own trigger and its own floor are anti-correlated: 29.5% of this week&rsquo;s session minutes cleared 22pt, and yet only <strong>11 of 24,121</strong> raw grind signals did. A gate that triggers on quiet absorption looks for exactly the minutes its floor excludes. Lowering the floor does not fix that &mdash; &sect;2b shows dropping it to 14pt adds 46 fires and makes the book worse. A floor at p90 is not a filter, it is an off-switch that opens during
violence, and it explains every grind number in this report: 24,110 signals blocked out of 24,121
this week, no live fire since 5 August, and two grind-exit shadow watches with nothing in them.</p>

<p class="ln">One more number worth having: <strong>31 of 33 days contain at
least one minute above the floor, but only 1 of 33 spend a quarter of the
session there.</strong> So &ldquo;the floor was reachable today&rdquo; is true almost every day and
means nothing; the floor is reachable for a few minutes of spike and then gone.</p>


