<p><strong>The ladder is monotone upward from 14pt, which is the shape of a real effect</strong> &mdash;
&minus;$1,143.50 &rarr; &minus;$799.50 &rarr; &minus;$554.50 &rarr; &minus;$514.50 &rarr; &minus;$280.50
&rarr; +$21.00 &rarr; +$347.50 &rarr; +$469.00. Three things follow, and none of them is the one the
report expected.</p>

<p><strong>One: 22 is not obviously wrong, and it is not enough.</strong> The live floor still leaves the
gate at <strong>&minus;$280.50 on 51 fires</strong>. It does not cross zero until 24pt and does not
clear it properly until 26&ndash;28. So the honest reading of the direction is &ldquo;higher would have
been better&rdquo;, not &ldquo;22 is a mistake&rdquo;.</p>

<p><strong>Two: no rung survives the battery.</strong> Strip the best three fires and <em>every</em> rung
is negative &mdash; &minus;$309.00 even at 28pt. The worst leave-one-day-out fold is negative at
<em>every</em> rung, &minus;$633.50 even at 28pt. And against 5,000 random same-size subsets of the same
128 fires, the 22pt rung sits at the 65th percentile and the 24pt rung at the 83rd &mdash; the floor is
selecting a little, and not much. <strong>Cause of death: strip-best-3 and leave-one-day-out, at every
rung tested.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Three, and this is the part that changes the Saturday question: the floor is not what is wrong
with grind.</strong> Rev1 framed it as the un-asked question on which the whole grind programme turns.
It has now been asked, and the answer is that raising the floor cannot make this gate positive on its
own live record. That does not make the shadow ladder pointless &mdash; it can still test whether the
same monotone shape holds forward &mdash; but it demotes it from &ldquo;the real grind question&rdquo; to
a confirmation, and it sends grind back to being an <em>entry</em> question, which is where
&sect;1&rsquo;s b- and c-family rows already pointed.</p>

