and $517.79 of it is the four gates that never fired at all.</p>


<div class="rev3"><div class="ct">&#9733; REV2 &mdash; THE &minus;$638.02 IS NOT <code>exhaustion_short</code>'S COUNTERFACTUAL, AND THE $782.52 IS NOT YOUR HAND</div>
<p>That row invited a reading the data does not support: that the operator turned a &minus;$638.02 gate
into a +$144.50 one. Two things are wrong with it, and the second is a method wound rather than an
arithmetic slip.</p>
<p><strong>One: repriced on the SAME 14 lots, the machine alone books +$84.00.</strong> Taking each
lot&rsquo;s protective stop straight off the venue (so R is exact, and 7 of 7 machine-closed lots
reproduce their own live exit reason) and racing it to its own configured stop or target, the machine
gets <strong>+$84.00</strong> and your hand adds <strong>+$60.50</strong>. Not $782.52. This gate is
<em>not</em> hand-carried, which is the opposite of what the same test says about
<code>abs_veto_short</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Two: only ONE of the 18 mechanical fires is a live signal.</strong> Matching the sim&rsquo;s
fires against the gate&rsquo;s seven live entries: one lines up within 3 seconds (08-18 13:37, and even
that one enters 8.75 points away), two more line up within 3 minutes at 17&ndash;33 points of
difference, and <strong>three live signals have no sim fire within TEN MINUTES</strong> &mdash;
14:02, 14:20 and 14:37 on 08-18, which are precisely the three that made money. Meanwhile the sim takes
17 fires the live gate never took. The contrast with <code>abs_veto_short</code> is the tell: for that
gate the sim reproduces <strong>11 of 12</strong> live signals within 6 seconds and about 5 points, so
its &minus;$749.55 <em>is</em> a counterfactual. <code>exhaustion_short</code> fires on a 5-second
footprint confirmation that does not replay faithfully off bars, so its &minus;$638.02 is a different
strategy&rsquo;s P&amp;L wearing the same name.</p>
<p><strong>What the row can honestly claim, then:</strong> most of the &minus;$638.02 is the value of the
router&rsquo;s benches (the gate was benched at 15:00Z Monday, 12:40Z Wednesday and 06:40Z Thursday, and
16 of those 17 never-live fires happened while it was), and <em>none</em> of it is a verdict on the seven
trades the gate actually took. Quote the ALL SIX total as a rough estimate of what the standdown was
worth, exactly as the box below already says &mdash; but do not read any single gate&rsquo;s row as that
gate&rsquo;s alternative history without first checking that the sim reproduces its fires.
<strong>BUILD&nbsp;#9 is the fix</strong>: populate <code>signal_journal.suppressed_by</code> so a refusal
is recorded rather than re-simulated.</p></div>
<div class="rev3"><div class="ct">★ AND HERE IS WHY THAT NUMBER IS AN ESTIMATE AND NOT A RESULT</div><p>The mechanical sim has no operator. Two of the live gates' best trades this week were closed by hand at a price the machine would not have taken, and the sim rides every one of those to its own stop. It also has no queue, no partial fills and no thin-tape veto. Every one of those biases makes the sim look WORSE than the live desk would have been, which means $1,846.86 is an upper bound on the value of benching, not a measurement of it.</p><p>The audit ran the same question on the fortnight with a different instrument and got a range from +$2,062 to −$673 — it straddles zero. <strong>So the correct statement is: the standdown was probably worth something this week, it was certainly not worth thousands, and no instrument on this desk can currently put a confident number on it.</strong> That is a measurement problem and it is on the BUILD card.</p></div>

<h3>4 &middot; ★ THE STANDDOWN IS A ROUTER-LOCAL VARIABLE, AND SOMETHING ELSE ARMED A GATE
THROUGH IT</h3>

<p>On Friday at 14:03Z, with the tournament stood down and all six gates reading <code>off</code>,
<code>abs_veto_short</code> was switched <strong>on</strong>. The router did not do it. Here is the
sequence, unedited, out of the trial log:</p>

<pre>2026-08-21T14:00:14Z | tune | DURABLE | changed: none | 14:00Z US-OPEN, tournament STOOD DOWN and all six gates already read off: no-change tick. Flat, healthy=True, halted=False; 
2026-08-21T14:03Z  APPLIED {abs_veto_short: on}  [OPEN-HOUR WATCHER, automatic]
2026-08-21T14:05:18Z | tune | DURABLE | changed: {&#x27;abs_veto_short&#x27;: &#x27;off&#x27;} | 14:05Z US-OPEN, tournament STOOD DOWN — abs_veto_short reads on because the OPEN-HOUR WATCHER auto-arme</pre>

<p class="ln"><code>open_hour_watch.py</code> is a second writer to <code>gate_switches.env</code>
with off&rarr;on authority and <strong>no awareness of the standdown at all</strong>. It saw a
confirmed break (ER 0.36, ATR 22, a new extreme) and did exactly what it was built to do, which is
the standing &ldquo;if a huge trend develops, arm immediately&rdquo; instruction. The problem is
not the watcher's logic. The problem is that a standing operator standdown lives in a variable
inside one process, and the other five writers to that file cannot see it.</p>

<p class="ln"><strong>The gate was live for 2 minutes 15 seconds</strong> and fired nothing, so
this cost $0 &mdash; by luck, not by design. The exposure is bounded by the router's 5-minute tick,
not by any guard. If the watcher had armed at 14:06 instead of 14:03 the gate would have been live
for the whole 5-minute window into the US open. <strong>The standdown needs to be a file the
writers check, not a variable one of them holds.</strong> That is a Saturday item.</p>

<p class="ln">The one genuinely reassuring thing here: the router caught it on its very next tick
and re-benched it, calling it &ldquo;housekeeping, not thrash&rdquo;. That is the behaviour you
want from the slow deliberate loop.</p>

