<div class="card"><h3>What the runs were &mdash; by cause, and what this week did to the vocabulary</h3><p>Each run gets a cause fingerprint from its pre-run tape. This used to be the map for where to hunt a new gate. <strong>This week three of the five labels were interrogated directly and did not survive it</strong> &mdash; the notes in the right-hand column are Movement&nbsp;3's findings, not decoration, and they are the reason no hunt should be scoped by a label again until the census is fixed.</p><table><tr><th>Cluster</th><th>Runs</th><th>What it is</th><th>What Movement 3 found</th></tr>
<tr><td>UNCLASS</td><td class="num">35</td><td>no clear tape tell (quiet ignition)</td><td>&#9733; base rate 75.4% on all tape, lift <strong>0.82</strong> &mdash; this is <em>coverage</em>, not a finding. Movement&nbsp;3 recommends renaming it <code>UNTESTED</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>OPEN/NEWS</td><td class="num">15</td><td>the 13:00&ndash;15:00 UTC cash-open / data window</td><td>&#9733; lift over the bare clock is <strong>0.921&times;</strong> &mdash; the two footprint tests remove 41% of the window and keep the <em>less</em> run-dense half</td></tr>
<tr><td>VACUUM</td><td class="num">13</td><td>moved AGAINST the tape (a snap-back / stop-run)</td><td>&#9733; <strong>this label is not a footprint</strong> &mdash; the flow event behind it fires on 20.4% of all tape and then splits it VACUUM/FLOW-LED on a 49.9% coin toss (Movement&nbsp;3, VACUUM &sect;2 and FLOW-LED)</td></tr>
<tr><td>FLOW-LED</td><td class="num">6</td><td>aggressors drove it (a continuation)</td><td>&#9733; same coin toss as VACUUM &mdash; the split carries 0.33pp of directional information; the lift it does carry is a VOLUME lift wearing a flow costume</td></tr>
<tr><td>VOL-EXPANSION</td><td class="num">1</td><td>amplitude broke out of a coil</td><td>not interrogated this week</td></tr>
</table></div>
<div class="callout"><div class="ct">HONEST MONEY &mdash; ceiling vs what we banked</div><table><tr><th></th><th>Runs</th><th>Hindsight ceiling</th><th>What we really made</th></tr><tr class="row-hl"><td>Caught (aligned)</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">$0</td><td class="num">+$0</td></tr><tr class="row-bad"><td>Fought (against)</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">$0</td><td class="num">$+0</td></tr><tr><td>Sat out</td><td class="num">70</td><td class="num">$11,401</td><td class="num">$0 &larr; the money on the table</td></tr></table><p>Every row is a sat-out row because there is no gate on this instrument to catch or fight anything. The ceiling is what one lot would have made on a perfect entry and a perfect exit, so treat it as the size of the seat, never as money forgone. The next two movements ask what could fill it.</p></div></section>
<hr class="frag-sep">
<section id="sec7"><div class="rev3"><div class="ct">&#9733; GAP &mdash; THIS SECTION WAS COMPUTED AGAINST THE SUPERSEDED CENSUS &mdash; its run list is 70/65, the rest of the report is 68/62</div><p>The census was re-frozen on 2026-08-25 with the run-picker's flow-bucket phase in a different place, and it moved: <strong>70 runs / 65 sat out / $9,986</strong> became <strong>68 runs / 62 sat out / $9,610</strong>. Movement&nbsp;1 and all seven of Movement&nbsp;3's hunts are on the new freeze. <strong>This section is on the old one</strong> &mdash; replaying eight gates across a week of tick data is a multi-hour job and it was not re-run. <strong>What that means for you:</strong> its headline finding is unaffected, because it does not depend on which particular runs are in the list &mdash; firing all six gates mechanically through this week's own tape loses &minus;$1,905.36 on 117 fires either way, and that is a statement about the GATES, not about the census. What you should NOT do is take a per-run number from this section and line it up against a per-run number in Movement&nbsp;1 or Movement&nbsp;3; three of the runs are not the same runs. &#9733; And note the cause: Movement&nbsp;3 found that <strong>21 of 68 labels change on bucket phase alone</strong>, which is the bug behind this divergence and is on the Monday list to fix.</p></div><h2 id="m2"><span class="n">M2</span> THE IDLE-GATE LAB &mdash; could the gates we already own
have caught them?</h2>

<p class="lead">Garrath &mdash; Movement&nbsp;1 found 65 big runs this week that the desk
sat out completely: 4,994 points of movement, worth $9,987.00 if they had been caught perfectly.
The obvious question is whether the six gates we already own would have caught any of them if
they had simply been left armed. <strong>The answer is a clean and rather uncomfortable no.</strong>
Fired mechanically into those runs, in their exact live configuration, they lose
$1,333.85. Fired across the whole week rather than only near the runs, they lose
$1,905.36. <strong>Not arming them was worth more than any entry idea in this
report.</strong></p>

<h3>1 &middot; The method, and the one thing that makes it honest</h3>

<p>Each of the 65 sat-out runs gets four independent simulations: the six gates fired
mechanically in the <strong>10 minutes before</strong> the run ignited (which is
the interesting one &mdash; catching a run means being in before it goes), and in the
<strong>5 minutes after</strong>; each of those both in the exact live config
and <strong>ungated</strong>, meaning every ATR floor, veto and confirmation stripped out. Exits
are the live <code>scaleout</code> slate plus <code>exit_overrides.json</code>, raced on real
ticks at $1.50 per round trip and $2.00 a point.</p>

<p>The thing that makes it honest is the <strong>base-rate control</strong>. Measuring a gate only
in windows we already know contained a big run is hindsight selection &mdash; it counts the fires
where the move existed and never counts the ones where it did not. So the same six gates are also
fired across the <em>entire</em> captured week, every fire counted. That control is the reason this
section can say something the last three idle-gate labs could not.</p>

<h3>2 &middot; The four arms</h3>

<table><thead><tr><th class="ln">Arm</th><th class="num">Runs it showed up for</th><th class="num">Fires</th><th class="num">Net</th><th class="num">$ / fire</th><th class="num">Win %</th><th class="num">Best fire stripped</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">10 min BEFORE ignition &middot; live config</td><td class="num">18 / 65</td><td class="num">23</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,333.85</td><td class="num">&minus;$57.99</td><td class="num">13%</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,684.85</td></tr><tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">10 min BEFORE &middot; every suppressor removed</td><td class="num">33 / 65</td><td class="num">60</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,620.40</td><td class="num">&minus;$27.01</td><td class="num">23%</td><td class="num">&minus;$1,971.40</td></tr><tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">5 min AFTER ignition &middot; live config</td><td class="num">13 / 65</td><td class="num">17</td><td class="num">&minus;$45.00</td><td class="num">&minus;$2.65</td><td class="num">41%</td><td class="num">&minus;$317.00</td></tr><tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">5 min AFTER &middot; ungated</td><td class="num">24 / 65</td><td class="num">49</td><td class="num">&minus;$626.75</td><td class="num">&minus;$12.79</td><td class="num">31%</td><td class="num">&minus;$898.75</td></tr></tbody></table>

<p class="ln">Every arm loses. Read the first two rows together: with their live filters on, the
gates show up for 18
of the 65 runs and lose $1,333.85; strip every filter off and they show up for
33 runs, take
60 fires, and lose
$1,620.40. <strong>Removing the
suppressors triples the activity and makes the result worse</strong>, which is the strongest
defence of the gates' own filters this desk has produced.</p>

<p class="ln"><strong>&#9733; REV2 &mdash; THE NEARLY-FLAT ROW IS ONE GATE.</strong> Split the post-ignition live arm by gate and <code>abs_veto_short</code> takes 5 fires for <strong>+$504.50 at a 100% win rate</strong> (+$97.00, +$97.00, +$97.00, +$102.50, +$111.00, on five distinct runs) while the other five gates take 12 fires for <strong>&minus;$549.50</strong>. Strip <code>abs_veto_short</code>'s single best fire and the whole arm goes from &minus;$45.00 to <strong>&minus;$156.00</strong>. So &ldquo;joining a run is roughly free&rdquo; is not a property of the roster; it is one gate on five fires, and every other gate is as bad after ignition as before. <strong>BUILD&nbsp;#6’s n&ge;60 kill criterion has to be PER GATE, not pooled</strong> &mdash; pooled, five lucky <code>abs_veto_short</code> fires would carry a shadow arm that five other gates are losing money in. The original observation, kept because the asymmetry is real for that one gate: fired <em>after</em> ignition with
live filters on, the gates lose only
$45.00 across
17 fires. Joining a run once it has already started is roughly free;
predicting one costs money. That is the same shape the greenfield lab reports from a completely
different direction in Movement&nbsp;3.</p>

