<div class="rev3"><div class="ct">★ THE SURVIVORSHIP TRAP THIS LAB EXISTS TO AVOID — and it cuts BOTH ways</div><p>Last cycle's idle-gate lab reported a small positive and it was survivorship: the gates looked good because of which ones happened to be idle in the windows chosen. This week the base-rate control was built specifically to close that, and it does — but note that it closes the flattering reading AND the damning one.</p><p>In the run windows the gates make <strong>&minus;$57.99 a fire</strong>. Across the whole week they make <strong>&minus;$16.29 a fire</strong>. <strong>&#9733; REV2 &mdash; Rev1 called those &ldquo;close&rdquo; and concluded there is nothing special about the tape near a big run. That is the one reading the numbers do not support, and it is wrong in the direction that makes the bench case STRONGER.</strong> The gap is <strong>3.56&times;</strong> and the run windows are the worse side. They are also not independent samples: <strong>21 of the 23 run-window fires sit inside the 117-fire base-rate set</strong> (same gate, same second), so the base rate mostly contains the thing it is being compared against. Remove them and the rest of the week is <strong>&minus;$4.49 a fire on 96 fires</strong> &mdash; a <strong>12.9&times;</strong> gap.</p><p>Bootstrapped over 20,000 resamples, the difference in $/fire is <strong>&minus;$41.71, 95% CI [&minus;$72.15, &minus;$2.52]</strong> against the full base rate (98.0% of resamples put the run windows worse), and <strong>95% CI [&minus;$82.41, &minus;$15.44]</strong> against the disjoint remainder (99.6%). Both intervals exclude zero. So the sentence Rev1 explicitly ruled out is the true one: <strong>these gates are substantially worse in the ten minutes before a big run than on ordinary tape.</strong> They fire into the chop that precedes an ignition and get stopped in it &mdash; which is exactly what &sect;4's direction table says from the other side. That is not a blur, it is the damning case, and it is the strongest form of the argument for leaving them benched.</p><p><strong>The honest limit on the headline, stated plainly:</strong> the &minus;$1,905.36 whole-week total has a bootstrap 95% CI on its per-fire mean of [&minus;$35.30, +$3.94], which scaled to 117 fires is roughly [&minus;$4,130, +$461] &mdash; it straddles zero. The TOTAL is confidently negative only as a point estimate; what is statistically solid here is the <em>comparison</em> (run windows worse than base rate, both CIs excluding zero), not the size of the prize. Quote &minus;$1,905.36 as the point estimate it is, never as a measured saving. So the conclusion is narrow and should not be over-read: these six gates, at these settings, on THIS week's tape, do not contain a run-catcher. It is one week, the desk was stood down for most of it, and a genuine trend week could look different. What it does license is leaving them benched for now, which is what is already happening.</p></div>

<h3>5 &middot; What this section does NOT say</h3>

<p>It does not say the $9,987.00 in those 65 runs is unreachable &mdash; Movement&nbsp;3
takes a different route at it and finds a size threshold that pays. It does not say the gates are
worthless; two of them traded live this week and one of them was green. And it emphatically does
not say &ldquo;arm nothing forever&rdquo;: it says that <em>this particular six-gate roster, fired
mechanically, is not the tool for catching runs</em>, and that the tool will have to be built
rather than switched on.</p>

