<h3 id="m3-bugs">7. Seven things the labs caught in their own instruments</h3>
<p>Every one of these was found by the lab that would have benefited from not finding it, and reported anyway. Read this section as the reason to trust the nulls above.</p>
<table><tr><th>What</th><th>Where</th><th>What happened</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr><tr><td><strong>The trail that never trailed</strong></td><td><span class='why'>RIDER_ALL &sect;3</span></td><td>The chandelier family survived every robustness test in the phase &mdash; until the trail was found to be broken. Corrected, <strong>+$4,270 becomes &minus;$631</strong>.</td><td>It would have shipped. Every test we own passed it, because every test was reading the same wrong simulator.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>A look-ahead of my own</strong></td><td><span class='why'>MGC &sect;7</span></td><td>A volatility cell read <strong>+$71,608</strong> until it was noticed that it entered at the OPEN of the bar whose HIGH it had already used. Corrected, it reads <strong>&minus;$12,462</strong>.</td><td>That number would otherwise have been the largest in this report.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Five shorts into one impulse</strong></td><td><span class='why'>VACUUM &sect;3</span></td><td>The first pass let overlapping entries run concurrently. Five shorts into the single 2026-07-31 impulse were worth $3.9k of a $10.3k headline. Scored one position at a time &mdash; the only way a live gate can trade &mdash; <strong>VAC-IGN-BRK falls from +$10,316 / +$15.49 per trade to +$911.55 / +$3.21</strong>.</td><td>Concurrency is not a backtest convenience; it is leverage the desk cannot take.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>A slice is not a filter</strong></td><td><span class='why'>UNCLASS &sect;3</span></td><td>Testing a filter by SLICING the trade list of an unfiltered rider gives +$28.33/tr. Running the identical filter INSIDE the simulation &mdash; where refusing a fire frees you to take the next one &mdash; gives <strong>+$5.68/tr, and n doubles</strong>.</td><td>Wrong by a factor of five, and slicing is the natural way to test a filter.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>A backward shift is an oracle, not a placebo</strong></td><td><span class='why'>OPEN/NEWS &sect;15</span></td><td>A signal shifted BACKWARDS in time is a look-ahead oracle. Only FORWARD (stale) shifts are legitimate placebos. Run the wrong way, it produces a false KILL.</td><td>Reported because it was run the wrong way first. The harness passed the leak-check: look-ahead earns 2.7&times; the real signal, as it should.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Leave-one-day-out cannot fail when k days carry the money</strong></td><td><span class='why'>OPEN/NEWS &sect;15</span></td><td>MOMBRK passed <strong>21 of 21</strong> LODO folds while <strong>two of twenty-one days held 97% of its P&amp;L</strong>.</td><td>Always quote profitable-days-out-of-total beside a LODO result, and run leave-TWO-out when the day distribution is that skewed.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The lake grew a look-ahead landmine</strong></td><td><span class='why'>MGC &sect;1.2</span></td><td>New <code>backfill_{1min,5mins,1hour,1day}.parquet</code> files now sit beside the daily 5s files. <strong>Any query without a <code>timeframe</code> filter inherits up to a full day of look-ahead, on every day.</strong></td><td><code>gf_mgc_tape.load_l1()</code> is safe. <code>mgc_session_anchor.load()</code> is NOT. This is a FIX REQUIRED, not a note.</td></tr></table>
<h3 id="m3-gold">8. Gold, in its own right &mdash; and the week's single most expensive lesson</h3>
<p class="lead">Last Friday we shadowed a gold gate called <code>mgc_hole_break_fade</code>. It has been running live in the shadow book since Monday and <strong>it is losing money: &minus;$1,196 across 80 trades in seven days, every one of its three arms red.</strong> Replaying the same seven days in the lab gives <strong>+$512</strong>. That is not a small disagreement, and the answer turned out to be one fixable thing.</p>
<div class="callout"><div class="ct">&#9733;&#9733;&#9733; THE GATE IS BEING FED THE WRONG TAPE</div><p>The research builds its one-minute bars from the <strong>depth mid</strong> &mdash; the midpoint of the order book, which updates every 250&nbsp;milliseconds whether or not anything trades. The live service builds its bars from <strong>trades</strong>. Gold prints sparsely, so those are not the same series, and a sixty-minute high taken from one is not the sixty-minute high of the other. Hold absolutely everything else constant &mdash; same break rule, same book filter, same exit, same spread-honest race &mdash; and change only the input tape:</p><table><tr><th>the same rule, exit and race, on the six forward days both tapes cover</th><th>n</th><th>net</th><th>$/trade</th></tr><tr><td><strong>depth-mid bars</strong> (what the research measured)</td><td>56</td><td><strong>+$172</strong></td><td><strong>+$3.06</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>trade bars</strong> (what the live service actually reads)</td><td>53</td><td><strong>&minus;$475</strong></td><td><strong>&minus;$8.96</strong></td></tr></table><p><strong>The sign flips on the bar source alone</strong>, and the trade-bar number is the one the live shadow independently produced. So this is not a mystery and it is not a research failure &mdash; it is a plumbing mismatch between the lab and production, and the fix is mechanical: the service already holds a <code>DepthFeed</code> connection to gold's book (it needs it for the liquidity filter anyway), so it already has the bid and the ask. It just needs to build its minute bar from their midpoint instead of from trades. <strong>Until that is done I would not promote a single gold cell, and I am not proposing one.</strong></p></div>
<h4>The per-cell verdicts &mdash; MGC, $10.00 a point, $1.50 the round trip</h4>
<table><tr><th>Cell</th><th>Gate</th><th>n (29d)</th><th>net</th><th>$/trade</th><th>forward 7d</th><th>placebo</th><th>live shadow</th><th>Verdict</th><th>Note</th></tr><tr><td><strong>REVERSION-LONG</strong></td><td><code>mgc_hole_break_fade</code> &mdash; 60-min break DOWN into a liquidity hole &rarr; <strong>buy</strong></td><td><span class="num-cell">134</span></td><td>+$1,853</td><td>+$13.83</td><td>+$488 (+$15.73)</td><td>0/30 &#10004;</td><td>&minus;$102.50 (n=7)</td><td><span class="tag pill-shadow">SHADOW</span></td><td>lead candidate, <strong>blocked on the bar-source fix</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>REVERSION-SHORT</strong></td><td><code>mgc_hole_break_fade</code> &mdash; 60-min break UP into a liquidity hole &rarr; <strong>sell</strong></td><td><span class="num-cell">154</span></td><td>+$1,390</td><td>+$9.03</td><td>+$25 (<strong>+$0.65</strong>)</td><td>0/30 &#10004;</td><td>&minus;$153.50 (n=7)</td><td><span class="tag pill-shadow">SHADOW</span></td><td>same, plus a <strong>side-decay watch</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>MOMENTUM-LONG</strong></td><td><code>mgc_wall_break_go</code> &mdash; break UP through a defended level &rarr; buy</td><td><span class="num-cell">101</span></td><td>+$838</td><td>+$8.30</td><td>+$378 (+$9.44)</td><td><strong>29/30 &#10008;</strong></td><td>not deployed</td><td><span class="tag pill-shadow">SHADOW, on probation</span></td><td>2nd week failing the random-minute placebo</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MOMENTUM-SHORT</strong></td><td><code>mgc_wall_break_go</code> &mdash; break DOWN through a defended level &rarr; sell</td><td><span class="num-cell">88</span></td><td>+$669</td><td>+$7.61</td><td>+$315 (+$9.56)</td><td><strong>29/30 &#10008;</strong></td><td>not deployed</td><td><span class="tag pill-shadow">SHADOW, on probation</span></td><td>same</td></tr><tr><td><strong>double-hole arm</strong></td><td><code>mgc_hole_break_fade_dbl</code> &mdash; both reversion cells</td><td><span class="num-cell">200</span></td><td>+$3,304</td><td><strong>+$16.52</strong></td><td>+$656 (+$15.26)</td><td>0/30 &#10004;</td><td>not deployed</td><td><span class="tag pill-shadow">SHADOW</span></td><td><strong>promote to a full arm</strong> &mdash; behind the bar-source fix</td></tr><tr><td><strong>volatility cell</strong></td><td><code>mgc_straddle</code> &mdash; breakout either way out of a coil</td><td><span class="num-cell">3,945</span></td><td>&minus;$1,248</td><td>&minus;$0.32</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>control also negative</td><td>&mdash;</td><td><span class="tag pill-dontarm">REFUTED</span></td><td>on 320 days. Last week's <em>reason</em> was wrong; the verdict was right</td></tr></table>
<p><strong>The exit family every gold cell needs</strong> is the <strong>wide chandelier on a single lot</strong> &mdash; stop 3.0&nbsp;ATR, arm 2.0, trail 2.0, 480-minute backstop. Not the tight scalp (negative at 1R on both entries). Not the dual slot (Lot&nbsp;A is negative on gold and cancels Lot&nbsp;B, on both entries). Not a time cap (its headline numbers are a long-drift artefact). &#9733; <strong>That is the exact opposite of the live MNQ slot configuration, and it replicates on 29 days.</strong> Gold is not the Nasdaq with a different multiplier, and the standing rule that MGC gates must be invented fresh rather than ported is earning its keep.</p>
<div class="card"><h3>Three more things gold produced this week</h3><p><strong>1. A year of tape arrived.</strong> The lake gained <strong>320 trading days</strong> of MGC minute bars (2025-07-28 &rarr; 2026-08-19) against last week's twenty-nine. It kills last week's router rule outright &mdash; that rule would bench the gate in NORMAL_CHOP, which is the regime currently paying best &mdash; closes last week's one loose thread, and puts a much colder light on the bare trigger (direction is real on 320 days; the P&amp;L is not).</p><p><strong>2. The unturned stone paid, and it is a VETO not a confirm.</strong> Gold against the Nasdaq on the same clock, over <strong>278 paired days</strong>: fading a gold break that the Nasdaq <em>agrees</em> with loses <strong>$10.52 a trade</strong> and sits at the 2.5th percentile of its own placebo. As an arm it reads +$5.43/tr against +$1.72 blanket. It does <em>not</em> reproduce on the 29-day book window (34th percentile), so it is a shadow arm with a question attached, not a promotion. The mirror &mdash; a cross-asset momentum <em>confirm</em> &mdash; is cleanly negative (&minus;$2.29/tr on the same 278 days), which is what a real asymmetry looks like.</p><p><strong>3. A look-ahead in the lab's own harness, caught before it reached you.</strong> A volatility cell read <strong>+$71,608</strong> until the author noticed it was entering at the open of the bar whose high it had already used. Corrected: <strong>&minus;$12,462</strong>. That number would otherwise have been the biggest in this report.</p></div>
