<h4>GREENFIELD SYNTHESIS — ONE RIDER FOR EVERY SAT-OUT RUN</h4>
<h6>MNQ · full parquet lake (5s bars 2026-07-16 → 08-24) + a 236-session out-of-sample leg · $2.00/point · $1.50 per round trip</h6>
<hr class="frag-sep">
<h5>THE SHORT VERSION, BEFORE ANY OF THE WORKING</h5>
<p>Garrath — I built the direction-agnostic run-catcher you asked for, boarded it late exactly as the gold work suggested, swept the exit far wider than the desk has ever run one, filtered it with a causal rule, and put the survivor through the whole battery. It passed every in-sample test we have, including two separate placebo controls. Then it died on the out-of-sample leg, and it died again on a live shadow arm that has been quietly recording forward trades since Monday.</p>
<p><strong>It died at STEP 3 — the causal filter. The test that killed it was the out-of-sample leg: 1,289 trades over 236 untouched sessions for a total of +$14.</strong> Not a loss. Not a win. Nothing. The 95% confidence interval on its expectancy runs from −0.204R to +0.246R per trade, which is another way of saying we measured zero with a wide ruler.</p>
<p>And there is a second, independent witness that agrees, which is the part you should trust most. <strong><code>rider_w5</code> — the board arm this desk shipped into the shadow book on 2026-08-15 off last week's version of this very phase, quoted then at +$4,841 and +$19.29 a trade — has taken 51 forward trades since 08-17 and sits at −$1,471, or −$28.84 a trade, red on six of seven days and red in every single ATR bucket.</strong> Nobody had graded it. This section grades it.</p>
<p>Three things came out of the week that are worth more than the failed rider:</p>
<ol><li><strong>The $9,610 is not $9,610.</strong> Even a <em>perfect</em> late boarder — one that fires inside all 62 runs at a median three minutes in and then exits at the exact top of the move with no costs at all — can only reach <strong>$5,094, or 53% of the ceiling</strong>. Thirteen per cent of the runs are already finished by the time any honest detector can see them. The number the census puts on the table is a hindsight number and the rider's half of it is gone before we start.</li><li><strong>Detection is not our problem and never was.</strong> A five-minute boarding trigger fires inside <strong>62 of 62</strong> sat-out runs at a median of <strong>three minutes</strong> late, with a median <strong>41 points still ahead</strong>. It also fires 34 times a day on tape that goes nowhere. The gap is <em>selection</em>, not detection — which is exactly what the greenfield census concluded from the other direction.</li><li><strong>I found a backtest bug that was manufacturing a winner</strong>, and it is the kind that would have shipped. Details in §3. The whole chandelier family survived every robustness test I have until I fixed it, and then went from +$4,270 to −$631.</li></ol>
<p>Full verdict, dispositions and the one shadow proposal are at the bottom. Every dead candidate is named with its cause of death.</p>
<hr class="frag-sep">
<h5>0. THE ANCHOR — I REPRODUCED THE FROZEN CENSUS BEFORE TOUCHING ANYTHING</h5>
<p>I did not re-run <code>run_census.py</code>. I re-derived its run set from the lake with its own algorithm and checked it line by line against the frozen <code>census_stdout.txt</code>.</p>
<table><tr><th>check</th><th>result</th></tr><tr><td>runs found on the census window (08-17 00:00 → 08-21 20:59 UTC)</td><td class="num">68</td></tr><tr><td>rows matching the frozen file on time <strong>and</strong> direction <strong>and</strong> move</td><td><strong>68 / 68</strong></td></tr><tr><td>typical 15-min range / threshold</td><td>37pt / 55pt — identical</td></tr><tr><td>sat-out runs</td><td class="num"><strong>62</strong></td></tr><tr><td>sat-out ceiling</td><td class="num"><strong>$9,610</strong></td></tr></table>
<p>⚠ <strong>The brief I was handed says "60 runs, $8,025".</strong> That is last week's census. This week's frozen file says 62 runs and $9,610, and since the frozen file is the contract, every number below is scored against $9,610. Worth flagging because the two numbers are close enough to pass unnoticed.</p>
<p><strong>One small defect found in the census while anchoring to it.</strong> The census slides its 15-minute window by <em>array index</em>, not by clock. Where the 5s tape has holes the two drift apart, and only <strong>16 of the 62</strong> sat-out run timestamps are actually minute-aligned — the offsets run 10s, 15s, 25s … up to 55s. It changes no money, but a run in that table is located to about ±1 minute, not to the second, and anything that joins to those timestamps on an exact match will silently miss most of them. I snapped each run to the minute bar containing it and took start prices from the 5s bar at the exact census timestamp.</p>
<hr class="frag-sep">
<h5>1. STEP ONE — BOARD IT. WHEN COULD WE HAVE KNOWN, AND WHAT WAS LEFT?</h5>
<p>The instruction was to stop trying to predict a run and instead board it <em>after</em> it has proven itself, then measure what remains. On gold that shape fired a median 19 minutes into the top five runs with about 80% of the move still ahead. Here is the same curve for MNQ.</p>
<p>The boarding rule is deliberately dumb and direction-agnostic: at each completed one-minute bar, take the net move over the trailing <strong>W</strong> minutes; if it is at least <strong>k × ATR</strong> and the efficiency ratio over that window is at least <strong>e</strong>, board it in whichever direction the move went. ATR here is the mean one-minute high-low range over the last 20 minutes, which is the exact number the live router already prints, so anything built on it is deployable as written.</p>
<p>"What was left" is measured to the <strong>run's own terminus</strong> — the furthest the move got before giving back a full ATR — with no costs and no stop. It is a pure oracle: this is the most any rider could have taken, not what one would take.</p>
<h6>The boarding curve, across the 62 sat-out runs</h6>
