<p><strong>Read the top row properly, because it is the most encouraging number in this whole section and it is still not enough.</strong> A five-minute confirmation catches <em>every one</em> of the 62 runs we sat out, and it catches them fast — three minutes in at the median, ten minutes at the very worst. At that moment the median run still has 41 points and 63% of its move in front of it. Detection latency, on this trigger, is simply not the problem.</p>
<p>That directly contradicts the prior this desk has been carrying. Part 2.5's detection-latency finding — median 119 points already moved, median 16 points left, 12% capture — was measured on a <strong>30-minute</strong> window with net ≥ 80 and ER ≥ 0.35. That is a much heavier confirmation and it buys a much later seat. Shortening the window from 30 minutes to 5 moves the median from "16 points left" to "41 points left". <strong>The latency was a property of the instrument we were measuring with, not of the tape.</strong></p>
<p>Now the two hard limits the same table imposes:</p>
<ul><li><strong>13% of runs have nothing left at all.</strong> By the time even the fastest honest trigger fires, the move is done. Those runs are not catchable by any boarding strategy, only by a predicting one, and predicting run starts is already a banked null on 22.6M ticks.</li><li><strong>The oracle ceiling is 53%, not 100%.</strong> $5,094 of the $9,610, with a cheating exit and zero costs. Tighten the trigger to something you would actually trade and it falls to 20–24%. Every honest conversation about this money should start from about <strong>$2,000–$5,000 a week</strong>, not $9,610.</li></ul>
<p>And the thing the table does not show: <strong>that same W=5 / k=1.0 trigger fires 34 times a day.</strong> Over the 35-day lake it produces 1,180 boards, of which about 60 a month are these runs. The other 96% are ordinary tape. That is the whole problem, and it is where the rest of this section goes.</p>
