<h5>2. STEP TWO — THE EXIT. SWEPT WIDE, AND THEN SWEPT AGAIN ON A SAMPLE TEN TIMES BIGGER</h5>
<p>The instruction was explicit: sweep the exit wide <em>before</em> concluding any entry fails, because three separate results this week said a tight exit gets shaken out of a grind while the same entries with a wide stop and a far target make money.</p>
<p>I did that first on the 35-day 5s lake. Entry set held fixed (W=8, k=1.5, ER≥0.50, 60-minute lockout, 1,034 boards) so that every cell differs in the exit and nothing else.</p>
<h6>Stop × target, net $ over the 35-day lake, 1 lot, $1.50/RT</h6>
