<p><strong>On the surface, the wide-exit thesis is confirmed.</strong> The live-desk-shaped exit (1.5 stop, 3.0 target) loses $783. Widen the stop to 4× ATR and push the target to 6–8× and the same entries make +$3,484 to +$3,495. That is a swing of over $4,200 on identical boards, purely on the exit — the same shape as the exhaustion_short rehab.</p>
<p><strong>But look at the surface, not the best cell.</strong> At a 4.0× stop, the target-3.0 cell loses $1,792, the target-4.0 cell loses $318, and the target-6.0 cell makes $3,484. A real edge does not do that. That is not a plateau, it is a lottery, and it is the first sign that a handful of trades are carrying everything.</p>
<p>Hold-to-time-cap variants (no target, flat at 15 / 30 / 60 / 240 minutes) were negative almost everywhere — that column is the right-hand edge of the table above.</p>
<h6>⚠ THE GRAVE THAT MATTERS MOST — a backtest bug that manufactured a winner</h6>
<p>The chandelier family — wide stop, trail armed after N×ATR, give back k×ATR from the peak — came out of this sweep looking like the answer. It was the <em>only</em> exit family positive in nearly every regime cell across two different entry definitions, and <code>chand_2.5_a1.5_t1.5</code> on the W=10 entry set survived everything I threw at it: +$4,270 net, +$4.58 a trade, 60% win, strip-best-3 still +$3,115, strip-best-<strong>10</strong> still +$1,098, day-green 61%, worst day −$631.</p>
<p>It was wrong. <strong>My trail was testing the bar's HIGH against the running peak instead of its LOW.</strong> A 5s bar that printed a new high and then collapsed registered a give-back of exactly zero, so the trail never fired inside the bar that actually broke it — it exited later, at prices that never traded. The peak was also read including the current bar, which is a second look-ahead on top of the first.</p>
<p>The tell was there before I found it, and it is worth naming so we catch the next one faster: <strong>the random-entry placebo.</strong> Firing at completely random minutes in a random direction with that same chandelier made <strong>+$1,542 on average</strong>, and the "real" rider's +$4,270 sat at p = 0.115 against that null. A random entry should not make money. When your null hypothesis starts printing, the bug is in the machinery, not in the market.</p>
<p>Fixed — peak taken from strictly prior bars, give-back tested against the adverse edge — here is what happened to the whole family:</p>
<table><tr><th>exit</th><th>entry set</th><th>BEFORE the fix</th><th>AFTER the fix</th></tr><tr><td>chand 2.5 / arm 1.5 / trail 1.5</td><td>W=10 k2.0</td><td>+$4,270 (strip-10 +$1,098)</td><td><strong>−$631</strong> (strip-10 −$3,339)</td></tr><tr><td>chand 3.0 / arm 2 / trail 2</td><td>W=10 k2.0</td><td>+$4,126 (strip-10 −$331)</td><td><strong>−$820</strong> (strip-10 −$4,491)</td></tr><tr><td>chand 3.0 / arm 2 / trail 2</td><td>W=8 k1.5</td><td class="num">+$3,026</td><td class="num"><strong>−$1,471</strong></td></tr><tr><td>chand 4.0 / arm 2 / trail 3</td><td>W=8 k1.5</td><td>+$5,247 (strip-10 −$736)</td><td><strong>−$872</strong> (strip-10 −$6,748)</td></tr><tr><td>chand 3.0 / arm 3 / trail 2</td><td>W=5 k1.0</td><td class="num">+$6</td><td class="num"><strong>−$4,728</strong></td></tr></table>
