<h6>The honest sweep — every cell judged, not just the best one</h6>
<p>Cherry-picking the best cell out of a grid is how a desk convinces itself. So I ran the full grid — 5 entry windows × 6 thresholds × 4 ER floors × 2 lockouts × 8 exits = <strong>1,824 scored cells</strong> — and put the <em>same battery</em> on every one of them: net &gt; 0, strip-best-3 &gt; 0, strip-best-10 &gt; 0, and <strong>both halves of the tape positive</strong>, with n ≥ 60.</p>
<table><tr><th>bar</th><th>cells passing</th><th>share</th></tr><tr><td>net &gt; 0</td><td>423 / 1,824</td><td class="num">23.2%</td></tr><tr><td>second half of the tape &gt; 0</td><td><strong>36 / 1,824</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>2.0%</strong></td></tr><tr><td>net <strong>and</strong> strip-3 <strong>and</strong> strip-10 <strong>and</strong> both halves</td><td><strong>5 / 1,824</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>0.3%</strong></td></tr></table>
<p>Two per cent of the entire configuration space makes money in the second half. If the edge were real and broad you would expect most of the 423 profitable cells to also be profitable in the back half; instead 423 shrinks to 36. With a 2% base rate you would expect about 8 of the 423 to clear the full bar by luck alone. <strong>Five did.</strong> The five "survivors" of the blanket sweep are exactly what chance hands you, and quoting any one of them would be a lie of selection.</p>
<h6>But the halves test is confounded, and I checked</h6>
<p>Before accepting "the edge decayed", I looked at what the two halves actually are.</p>
<table><tr><th>half</th><th>dates</th><th>sessions</th><th>ATR20 median</th><th>mean daily range</th><th>|15-min move| p90</th><th>ER30 median</th></tr><tr><td>first</td><td>07-16 → 08-04</td><td class="num">14</td><td class="num"><strong>13.9pt</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>709pt</strong></td><td class="num">67.1pt</td><td class="num">0.172</td></tr><tr><td>second</td><td>08-05 → 08-24</td><td class="num">14</td><td class="num"><strong>9.3pt</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>398pt</strong></td><td class="num">44.4pt</td><td class="num">0.156</td></tr></table>
<p>The second half is a <strong>33% quieter tape by ATR and 44% quieter by daily range</strong>. So "it stopped working in half two" and "it stops working when volatility halves" are the same sentence, and the blanket halves test cannot tell them apart. That is precisely the failure mode the desk's own backtest discipline warns about — a blanket cross-tape number averages the regimes a config should be ON with those it should be OFF, and is a bug. So I split by regime first.</p>
<hr class="frag-sep">
<h5>3. STEP THREE — THE FILTER, AND THE ONE THAT ACTUALLY WORKED</h5>
<h6>Regime first, config second</h6>
<p>I bucketed every board by the <strong>ATR level at entry</strong> — causal, known before the trade, and the live router's own meter — and re-ran a 216-cell entry × exit sweep <em>inside each bucket</em>, requiring both halves of that bucket to be positive.</p>
<table><tr><th>ATR20 at entry</th><th>cells scored</th><th>net &gt; 0</th><th>2nd half &gt; 0</th><th>pass both halves + strip-3</th></tr><tr><td><strong>ATR &lt; 9</strong> (quiet / dead)</td><td class="num">216</td><td>23 (11%)</td><td>15 (7%)</td><td><strong>4 (2%)</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>ATR 9–14</strong> (the desk's normal tape)</td><td class="num">216</td><td>188 (87%)</td><td>75 (35%)</td><td><strong>66 (31%)</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>ATR ≥ 14</strong> (violent)</td><td class="num">216</td><td>81 (38%)</td><td>39 (18%)</td><td><strong>16 (7%)</strong></td></tr></table>
<p>A fifteen-fold difference in survival rate between buckets is not something chance produces evenly. Something real lives in the middle band, and the blanket number had been averaging it away.</p>
<h6>…but first, is that just leverage?</h6>
<p>Stops and targets are ATR-scaled, so a higher-ATR trade is mechanically a bigger bet in dollars. If the "filter" simply selects bigger bets, dollars per trade rises while the actual edge stays flat, and the rule is a position-sizing choice dressed up as an insight. So I rescored everything in <strong>R</strong> — net P&amp;L divided by (ATR at entry × $2) — which is the same trade measured in units of what it risked.</p>
<table><tr><th>ATR20 band</th><th>W8 k2.5 (R/trade)</th><th>W10 k2.5 (R/trade)</th><th>W15 k3.0 (R/trade)</th><th>agreement</th></tr><tr><td>0–7</td><td class="num">+0.07</td><td class="num">+0.45</td><td class="num">−0.12</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td>7–9</td><td class="num">+0.27</td><td class="num">−0.63</td><td class="num">−0.24</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td>9–11</td><td class="num">+0.48</td><td class="num">−0.38</td><td class="num">+0.26</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><strong>11–13</strong></td><td class="num">+0.07</td><td class="num"><strong>+0.66</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>+1.03</strong></td><td>partial</td></tr><tr><td><strong>13–16</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>+1.42</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>+1.09</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>+0.87</strong></td><td><strong>all three</strong></td></tr><tr><td>16+</td><td class="num">−0.11</td><td class="num">+0.19</td><td class="num">−0.11</td><td>no (all ≈ 0)</td></tr></table>
<p>In R the relationship is <strong>not</strong> monotone in ATR, which kills the leverage explanation. It is a <strong>band</strong>: the money is between roughly 11 and 16 points of ATR, and both edges matter. Below it the moves are too small relative to noise and the round trip; above it the tape is violent enough that a "proven" move is more often an exhausted one — which is the same thing the abs_veto rehab found when the amp_floor let through elevated-ATR-but-spent overnight thrusts.</p>
<h6>The candidate</h6>
<blockquote><strong>RIDER_ALL / <code>board_atrband</code></strong> — sampled at each completed one-minute bar. ATR20 = mean 1-min high−low over the last 20 minutes. <strong>Fire when 11 ≤ ATR20 &lt; 16 AND |net over 10 min| ≥ 2.5 × ATR20 AND ER(10 min) ≥ 0.50.</strong> Direction = sign of the 10-minute move. Stop 3.0 × ATR, target 6.0 × ATR, flat at 240 minutes, 60-minute lockout between fires.</blockquote>
