<h6>The controls</h6>
<table><tr><th>control</th><th>result</th><th>reading</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Direction flipped</strong> (same boards, opposite side)</td><td>−$2,436, −$16.68/tr, <strong>−0.637R</strong></td><td>direction is doing real work — it is not an exit artefact</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Random-time placebo</strong> — same ATR band, random minutes, random side, 400 draws</td><td>null mean +$229, sd $1,335 → <strong>p = 0.0125</strong></td><td>beats a random entry with the same exit</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Placebo cut</strong> — keep a random 146 of the 518 unfiltered fires, 4,000 draws</td><td>null mean +$1,088, sd $1,246 → <strong>p = 0.029</strong></td><td>the ATR band beats discarding the same <em>number</em> of fires at random</td></tr><tr><td>Unfiltered (all ATR)</td><td>518 trades, +$3,864, +$7.46/tr, +0.190R</td><td>the band roughly triples expectancy per trade</td></tr></table>
<h6>★ What the cut COSTS — the number I am obliged to report and it is not flattering</h6>
<p>Cutting to the ATR band discards <strong>372 of the 518 fires</strong>. Those 372 contain <strong>131 winners worth +$18,483</strong> which we forgo, and <strong>241 losers worth −$18,115</strong> which we avoid. Net effect of the entire filter on the bottom line: <strong>+$368.</strong></p>
<p>So the ATR band does <strong>not</strong> find money. It <em>concentrates</em> the same money into a quarter of the trades. That is genuinely worth something — it is what lifts $7.46 a trade to $23.95 and what makes the thing survive strip-10 — but anyone reading "+0.9R a trade" as "the filter found an edge" has it backwards. The filter found a smaller sample.</p>
<h6>And the caveat I have to put next to my own p-values</h6>
<p>I chose the band edges <em>after looking at the data</em>. There were roughly 36 plausible lo × hi combinations in the sweep I ran. A p of 0.029 against 36 looks, corrected, like a p of about 1.0. The placebo cut controls for "any cut of this size" but not for "I went looking for the best cut". The in-sample battery above is real and the controls are honest, but it is not, on its own, evidence that survives the multiplicity. Which is why the next section exists.</p>
