<p><strong>The overnight lead dies on replication, and it dies on the controls rather than on the headline.</strong> The rider itself keeps its sign in both samples for one particular parameterisation. But the <em>claim</em> — that overnight tape behaves differently from daytime tape for a boarding rider — is not reproduced: on the held-out lake daytime is just as good or better in five of six comparisons, and the counter-trend control, which was reassuringly negative out of sample, comes back <strong>positive</strong>. When your control flips sign, you are reading noise.</p>
<p>Two further honesty points. The session split was <strong>one of three post-hoc comparisons</strong>, so its nominal significance should be trebled. And the random-time placebo inside the same band <em>and</em> the same session gives <strong>p = 0.097</strong> — even on its own generating sample it is only about 1.3 standard deviations above firing at random times.</p>
<p><strong>It is not nothing, and I am not calling it dead.</strong> It has the right sign in two independent samples, its counter-trend leg is negative in both, and it is the only thing in this study that survives a full point of slippage. That earns incubation, not deployment. See the disposition table.</p>
<hr class="frag-sep">
<h5>7. THE STONE I HAD NOT TURNED — THE L2 BOOK AT THE BOARD MOMENT</h5>
<p>The census scores every run for far-side depletion, and I had used none of it. The obvious question: at the moment the board rule fires, does a thin far side — the side price is running <em>into</em> — tell us which of those 34 daily fires is a real run? We hold 264 million MNQ book rows from 07-31 to 08-24, so this is answerable with data we already have.</p>
<p>For each of the 311 fires inside the book window I took the far-side share of level-1-to-3 depth over the 30 seconds <strong>before</strong> entry (below 0.50 = the far side is thin = the run is telegraphed).</p>
<table><tr><th>far-side share</th><th>n</th><th>net $</th><th>$/trade</th><th>R/trade</th><th>win</th></tr><tr><td>0.00–0.42</td><td class="num">13</td><td class="num">−$38</td><td class="num">−$2.93</td><td class="num">−0.343</td><td class="num">31%</td></tr><tr><td>0.42–0.47</td><td class="num">44</td><td class="num">−$300</td><td class="num">−$6.82</td><td class="num">−0.544</td><td class="num">30%</td></tr><tr><td>0.47–0.50</td><td class="num">109</td><td class="num">−$680</td><td class="num">−$6.24</td><td class="num">−0.142</td><td class="num">35%</td></tr><tr><td>0.50–0.53</td><td class="num">89</td><td class="num">+$188</td><td class="num">+$2.11</td><td class="num">+0.139</td><td class="num">36%</td></tr><tr><td>0.53–0.58</td><td class="num">45</td><td class="num">−$379</td><td class="num">−$8.42</td><td class="num">−0.308</td><td class="num">33%</td></tr><tr><td>0.58–1.01</td><td class="num">11</td><td class="num">−$166</td><td class="num">−$15.09</td><td class="num">−0.662</td><td class="num">27%</td></tr></table>
<p><strong>A clean null.</strong> Correlation between far-side share and R is <strong>−0.009</strong> across 311 trades. Cutting to a thin far side gives −0.265R against −0.060R for a thick one — i.e. the wrong way round — and against a random cut of the same size the placebo p is <strong>0.62</strong>. There is no ordering, no monotonicity, no signal. The L2 book, read this way, at this moment, tells us nothing about which board becomes a run.</p>
<p>That closes the stone. It also means "we need a data source we do not capture" is not the excuse available here — we had the deepest data on the desk and it was empty.</p>
