<p>On the census week itself, running one position at a time, the candidate took <strong>42 trades and lost $245</strong>. It <em>did</em> show up — it participated in <strong>32 of the 62 sat-out runs</strong>, roughly half — and it still lost money, because the other trades cost more than the runs paid. That is the sharpest single sentence in this report: <strong>showing up to half the runs was not the hard part, and it did not help.</strong></p>
<p>The oracle table is the number to carry forward. This is a <strong>$2,000–$5,000-a-week oracle, not a $9,610-a-week one</strong>, and per the desk's own precedent that distinction changes what the money is worth building for. The gap between the $5,094 oracle and the $0 causal result is not an exit problem and not a detection problem — it is <strong>selection</strong>, and it is 100% of the gap.</p>
<p><strong>Why the mechanism fails, in one paragraph.</strong> At the moment the boarding rule is true, the median run has 12 to 41 points left depending on how much confirmation you demand — and the <em>same</em> rule is true 34 times a day on tape that does nothing. The trades that are not runs pay the stop, which at 3×ATR in the 11–16 band is 33 to 48 points. So the rider is risking 33–48 points to collect a median 12–41, and it must be right far more often than 44% of the time for that to work. It is not. The wide exit helps because it lets the occasional big run pay for many stops, but the ratio of runs to non-runs at the moment of firing — about 1 in 20 — is simply too thin. <strong>Boarding is solved. Selecting is not.</strong> That is the same conclusion the greenfield census reached from the other end when it found that a plain 3/6×ATR exit converts 40% of the ceiling but 105 of 155 fires are not runs at all.</p>
