<h5>11. THE SHADOW BLOCK — AND TWO THINGS THAT MUST BE BUILT FIRST</h5>
<p>⚠ <strong>This cannot be pasted in as-is.</strong> I checked the shadow book's fields against the rule and found two genuine blockers. Both are small, but shipping around either one would record a <em>different</em> strategy under this name — which is exactly how <code>rider_w5</code> came to be quoted at +$19.29 while making −$28.84.</p>
<ol><li><strong><code>gate_board</code> cannot express a wrapping clock window.</strong> It tests <code>hh_lo &lt;= utc_hour &lt; hh_hi</code>, which is never true for 21:00→07:00. Needs one line: <code>inw = (hh_lo &lt;= h &lt; hh_hi) if hh_lo &lt; hh_hi else (h &gt;= hh_lo or h &lt; hh_hi)</code>.</li><li><strong><code>ShadowVariant</code> has an <code>atr_max</code> but no <code>atr_min</code>.</strong> The rule is a <em>band</em>, and both edges carry weight (§3). An arm shipped with only the ceiling is a different arm. Needs one field, defaulted to 0.0 so nothing existing changes.</li><li><strong>Note on ER.</strong> <code>ShadowVariant.er_min</code> reads <strong>ER30</strong>, not the entry window's ER. Substituting ER30 flips this rule's sign on the held-out sample (§6), so the arm must gate on the <strong>10-minute</strong> ER. Either add <code>er_win_min</code> to the gate params or accept that an <code>er_min</code> arm is testing a different hypothesis and label it as such.</li></ol>
