<p><strong>NO RIDER SURVIVES. The pooled, direction-agnostic late-boarding run-catcher DIES AT STEP 3 — THE CAUSAL FILTER — killed by the OUT-OF-SAMPLE LEG: 1,289 trades across 236 untouched sessions for a total of +$14, a 95% confidence interval on expectancy of [−0.204R, +0.246R], and a per-trade result that goes negative on one tick of slippage. The verdict is independently corroborated by the live shadow arm <code>rider_w5</code>, which has taken 51 forward trades since 08-17 for −$1,471 (−$28.84/trade), red on six of seven days and red in every ATR bucket including the candidate's own — <code>rider_w5</code> is REFUTED and should be retired. Step 1 SUCCEEDED and should be believed: a 5-minute boarding trigger fires inside 62 of 62 sat-out runs at a median 3 minutes late with a median 41 points still ahead, so detection is solved. Step 2's wide-exit thesis is CONFIRMED IN DIRECTION but INSUFFICIENT IN SIZE — a wide stop and a far target reliably make this rider less negative and never make it positive across 42 out-of-sample exit cells. The binding constraint is SELECTION: the same trigger fires 34 times a day and roughly 1 fire in 20 is a run, so the rider risks a 33–48 point stop to collect a median 12–41 points. The honest prize is NOT $9,610 — a perfect late boarder with a cheating exit reaches $5,094 (53%), a deployable one reaches $1,910–$2,286 (20–24%), and 13% of the runs are finished before any honest trigger can see them. The causal result on the census week itself was −$245 across 42 trades while participating in 32 of the 62 runs. ONE lead goes forward: <code>board_overnight_band</code> to SHADOW as an instrumented question (+0.444R on 236 generating sessions, +0.224R held-out, but its daytime and counter-trend controls reverse and its placebo p is 0.097), gated behind two small code changes and a four-part promotion bar. The next attempt needs a SIZE PREDICTOR at the board moment, not more days, not a wider exit, and not a new data source — the L2 b