<p>Cumulatively: ≥55pt +$56.43/tr, ≥70pt +$65.50, ≥90pt +$77.27, ≥120pt +$109.81. <strong>The size threshold is real and it is around 130 points on MNQ</strong> — below that the runs pay for themselves and nothing more; above it they carry the entire book.</p>
<p><strong>But that is the PRIZE, not a RULE, and here is why.</strong> The rider cannot know the run's eventual size at the moment it boards. The obvious counter-argument is that it can: ATR at board time correlates +0.719 with eventual run size in points, and the 5-minute move correlates +0.851. Those numbers are a scale artefact and I nearly filed them as a finding. Run size is measured in points and so is volatility, so a loud day produces big runs by arithmetic. Normalise the run size by the ATR at board time and the correlations collapse:</p>
<table><tr><th>board-time read</th><th>vs run size in POINTS</th><th><strong>vs run size in ATRs</strong></th></tr><tr><td>ATR</td><td class="num">+0.719</td><td class="num"><strong>−0.221</strong></td></tr><tr><td>|5-min move|</td><td class="num">+0.851</td><td class="num"><strong>+0.133</strong></td></tr><tr><td>|5-min move| ÷ ATR</td><td class="num">+0.239</td><td class="num">+0.477</td></tr><tr><td>ER30</td><td class="num">+0.336</td><td class="num"><strong>+0.045</strong></td></tr></table>
<p>The only surviving read is the thrust size expressed in ATRs, at r=+0.48 — and that is already the <code>k</code> parameter, whose sweep in §5 shows no usable gradient. <strong>There is a size at which these runs are worth catching. There is no instrument on this desk that identifies it at board time.</strong></p>
<p>Big-moves-caught on the frozen census week: <strong><code>UNCL-RIDE-ER</code> boards 14 of the 41 UNCLASS runs, and its trades on that week are n=33, −$209, −$6.33 a trade.</strong></p>
