<hr class="frag-sep">
<h5>1. ★ INTERROGATING THE LABEL FIRST</h5>
<p>The brief says to check the cluster label's base rate before trusting anything built on top of it, and that a label true on most of the tape is noise, not a footprint — and that this finding outranks any signal built on top. I did it first. It changed where I was allowed to look.</p>
<h6>1.1 What the label is <em>now</em></h6>
<p>Last year's version of this page found <code>OPEN/NEWS</code> was literally <code>if 13 &lt;= hour &lt; 15</code> checked <strong>before</strong> every footprint test. That has been fixed. From <code>scripts/run_census.py:78-84</code>:</p>
<pre><code>def cluster(hour, flow, mv, amp, fz=None):
    if flow is not None and fz is not None and abs(fz) &gt;= FLOW_Z:
        return &quot;FLOW-LED&quot; if (flow &gt; 0) == (mv &gt; 0) else &quot;VACUUM&quot;
    if amp is not None and amp &gt; 0.30:
        return &quot;VOL-EXPANSION&quot;
    if 13 &lt;= hour &lt; 15:
        return &quot;OPEN/NEWS&quot;          # no footprint, but the densest window — say so
    return &quot;UNCLASS&quot;</code></pre>
<p>The clock now goes <strong>last</strong>. <code>OPEN/NEWS</code> is a <strong>residual</strong>, and the full predicate is a conjunction:</p>
<blockquote><code>13:00 ≤ hour &lt; 15:00 UTC</code> <strong>AND</strong> <code>|flow z| &lt; 1</code> (no aggressor-imbalance event in the prior 60s) <strong>AND</strong> <code>amp ≤ 0.30</code> (prior-5-min hi-lo ≤ 0.30% of price)</blockquote>
<p>That is a materially different object from a clock, and the docstring's own claim — that the window is worth keeping as a fallback — is testable. So I tested it.</p>
<h6>1.2 Base rate — measured on ALL bars, not on runs</h6>
<p>I re-implemented the census's run detector and its three feature functions (<code>flow_at</code>, <code>flow_z_at</code>, <code>amp</code>) against the <strong>full parquet lake</strong> at 5s — 438,253 bars, 2026-07-16 → 2026-08-21 — and scored the label on <strong>every</strong> 60s-step 15-minute window, run or not. 36,507 windows.</p>
<table><tr><th>cluster</th><th>windows</th><th>% of tape</th><th>runs (≥1.5×ATR)</th><th>P(run)</th><th>lift vs 1.109% base</th></tr><tr><td>UNCLASS</td><td class="num">28,007</td><td class="num">76.72%</td><td class="num">240</td><td class="num">0.857%</td><td class="num">0.77×</td></tr><tr><td>FLOW-LED</td><td class="num">2,988</td><td class="num">8.18%</td><td class="num">33</td><td class="num">1.104%</td><td class="num">1.00×</td></tr><tr><td>VACUUM</td><td class="num">2,901</td><td class="num">7.95%</td><td class="num">39</td><td class="num">1.344%</td><td class="num">1.21×</td></tr><tr><td><strong>OPEN/NEWS</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>1,900</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>5.20%</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>60</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>3.158%</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>2.85×</strong></td></tr><tr><td>VOL-EXPANSION</td><td class="num">711</td><td class="num">1.95%</td><td class="num">33</td><td class="num">4.641%</td><td class="num"><strong>4.18×</strong></td></tr></table>
<p>Two things fall straight out.</p>
<p><strong>First: the label is true on the majority of its own window.</strong> 3,239 windows sit inside 13:00–15:00Z; <strong>1,900 of them (58.66%) wear the label</strong>. The 08-15 fix took it from 100% of its window to 59% of its window. That is progress, and it is not enough to call it a footprint — a coin that lands heads 59% of the time inside its own domain is still mostly describing the domain.</p>
<p><strong>Second — and this is the one that matters:</strong></p>
<table><tr><th>slice of 13–15Z</th><th>windows</th><th>runs</th><th>P(run)</th><th>lift vs base</th></tr><tr><td>the bare clock, everything in 13–15Z</td><td class="num">3,239</td><td class="num">111</td><td class="num">3.427%</td><td class="num"><strong>3.09×</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>OPEN/NEWS</code> (clock ∧ flow-quiet ∧ amp-quiet)</td><td class="num">1,900</td><td class="num">60</td><td class="num">3.158%</td><td class="num">2.85×</td></tr><tr><td>what the label <em>throws away</em> (in-window, has a footprint)</td><td class="num">1,339</td><td class="num">51</td><td class="num"><strong>3.809%</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>3.43×</strong></td></tr></table>
<blockquote><strong>★ The label's lift OVER the bare clock is 0.921×.</strong></blockquote>
<p>The two footprint tests take a 3.427%-dense window, remove 41% of it, and keep the <em>less</em> dense half. The bit of the label that is not a clock is not merely uninformative — <strong>it is mildly negatively informative.</strong> Everything <code>OPEN/NEWS</code> knows about where runs happen, it knows because of the clock.</p>
<h6>1.3 Is that an artefact of the pre-tick era?</h6>
<p>The lake's ticks start 2026-07-24; before that <code>flow_z_at</code> returns <code>None</code> and the flow test cannot fire, so 07-16…07-23 windows fall through to the clock by default and could be inflating the label. Split it:</p>
<table><tr><th>era</th><th>windows</th><th>13–15Z P(run)</th><th><code>OPEN/NEWS</code> P(run)</th><th>label lift over clock</th><th>label share of its window</th></tr><tr><td>ALL LAKE 07-16→08-21</td><td class="num">36,507</td><td class="num">3.427%</td><td class="num">3.158%</td><td class="num"><strong>0.921×</strong></td><td class="num">58.66%</td></tr><tr><td>TICK ERA 07-24→08-21</td><td class="num">28,349</td><td class="num">3.373%</td><td class="num">3.190%</td><td class="num"><strong>0.946×</strong></td><td class="num">53.49%</td></tr></table>
<p>Same answer both ways. In the tick era the label covers 53% of its own window and still returns 0.946× the bare clock's lift. <strong>Not an artefact.</strong></p>
<h6>1.4 Which of the two footprint tests is doing it</h6>
<p><code>amp &gt; 0.30</code> is true on only <strong>3.04%</strong> of all windows — it barely bites. <code>|fz| ≥ 1</code> is true on <strong>16.13%</strong>. So essentially all of the subtraction is the flow test, and essentially all of the anti-lift is the flow test removing in-window runs that <em>did</em> have a flow footprint and were, on the evidence, slightly more likely to be runs than the ones left behind.</p>
<p>That is not a bug in <code>cluster()</code> — it is doing exactly what it says, routing footprinted runs to FLOW-LED/VACUUM so they reach the hunts that can use them. It <em>is</em> a warning about this bucket: what is left is, by construction, <strong>the runs for which the census's two instruments found nothing.</strong></p>
<h6>1.5 ★★ What that forces on the hunt</h6>
<p>This is the structural point of the whole section, and I want it stated plainly before any gate:</p>
<blockquote><strong><code>OPEN/NEWS</code> is a bucket defined by the absence of evidence.</strong> A run lands here <em>because</em> aggressor flow was unremarkable in the 60 seconds before it and the prior five minutes were not unusually wide. Any signal I build that uses aggressor flow or pre-run amplitude would, if it worked, have <em>relabelled the run out of the bucket</em>. The only channels that can legitimately catch an <code>OPEN/NEWS</code> run are the ones <code>cluster()</code> never reads: - <strong>price structure</strong> (ranges, levels, VWAP, momentum) — never read; - <strong>raw volume / trade count</strong> — never read (<code>flow</code> is the <em>net signed</em> aggressor sum; a two-sided auction has huge volume and ~zero net flow, and the label cannot tell it from a dead tape); - <strong>L2 book</strong> — the census <em>measures</em> <code>book_depletion()</code> and prints it, but <code>cluster()</code> never receives it. Free channel. - cross-asset — out of scope for this section.</blockquote>
<p>Those three are exactly what §4–§7 test, in that order. Nothing here is a flow gate, deliberately.</p>
<h6>1.6 A free recommendation for the census</h6>
<p><code>VOL-EXPANSION</code> covers <strong>1.95% of the tape at 4.18× run lift</strong> — the highest of any cluster, better than <code>OPEN/NEWS</code>'s 2.85× on 5.20%, and it is the label the census currently produces least often (1 run in the frozen week, 33 across the lake). It is checked <em>second</em>, so a run that also has a flow footprint never reaches it. If a bucket is going to route next quarter's hunt, that is the one with the sharpest edge per unit of tape, and its <code>amp &gt; 0.30</code> threshold — true on 3.04% of tape — is worth sweeping. Not my brief; recorded because I measured it.</p>
