<h6>6.3 ★ Why leave-one-day-out passed and should not have reassured anyone</h6>
<p><strong>All 21 LODO folds are positive. Two days carry 97% of the P&amp;L.</strong> Both statements are true at once, and that is a property of the test, not of the gate: with 21 days and 2 money days, removing one day <em>always</em> leaves the other. <strong>LODO with k money days cannot fail until you leave k days out.</strong> I therefore ran leave-two-out — 210 folds — and the worst fold (drop 07-27 and 07-31 together) collapses to <strong>+$1.19/trade</strong>, i.e. break-even.</p>
<p>That is worth carrying forward as a rule: <strong>quote the number of profitable days alongside any LODO result.</strong> 7-up/14-down with a passing LODO is a warning, not a clearance.</p>
