<h5>7. ★ THE ROUTER — what would be armed, and the honest answer that nothing is</h5>
<p>The mandate is explicit that a blanket cross-tape number is a bug and that a surviving gate must come with its home regime and an arm rule in the live router's vocabulary. <strong>Nothing survived</strong>, so what follows is the segmentation evidence and the rule that <em>would</em> have been written, stated so it is falsifiable rather than aspirational.</p>
<p>The regime split is consistent across all three candidates and is the one genuine policy finding here — <strong>every one of them is an ATR-high, US-session animal and every one of them bleeds in low ATR</strong>:</p>
<table><tr><th></th><th>VAC-ABS</th><th>VAC-SNAP</th><th>VAC-IGN-BRK</th></tr><tr><td>ATR low tercile</td><td>−$5.26/tr (n=33)</td><td>−$8.10/tr (n=137)</td><td>−$11.64/tr (n=95)</td></tr><tr><td>ATR mid tercile</td><td>−$5.99/tr (n=31)</td><td>−$10.09/tr (n=136)</td><td>+$3.24/tr (n=94)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ATR high tercile</strong></td><td><strong>+$50.86/tr (n=33)</strong></td><td><strong>+$20.82/tr (n=137)</strong></td><td><strong>+$18.03/tr (n=95)</strong></td></tr><tr><td>US session 13:00–21:00Z</td><td>+$51.24/tr (n=26)</td><td>+$22.89/tr (n=104)</td><td>+$16.33/tr (n=110)</td></tr><tr><td>overnight / pre-open</td><td>−$0.18/tr (n=71)</td><td>−$6.57/tr (n=306)</td><td>−$5.08/tr (n=174)</td></tr></table>
<p><strong>ER is not a discriminator here, in either direction.</strong> Serially, VAC-IGN-BRK books −$0.49/tr in dead chop (ER15 &lt; 0.15, n=83), <strong>+$17.85/tr in the 0.15–0.35 middle</strong> (n=93) and −$6.55/tr in clean trend (n=108) — a middle bucket carrying the whole of a $911 net across three buckets is noise, not a regime. ⚠ Recorded because it matters: the <em>unconstrained</em> run said +$22.28 in chop and +$13.79 in trend, and that was the overlap-inflated ledger — exactly the number this section warns against quoting.</p>
<p>What <strong>is</strong> a router finding, and it comes from the detection work in §5 rather than from any backtest: the biggest runs <strong>start from chop</strong> — ER15 is the only feature measured whose AUC sits <em>below</em> 0.5, and it falls further as the runs get bigger (<strong>0.442</strong> on the sat-out 65, <strong>0.403</strong> on the top-15). Every ER-floor arm condition the live router owns is standing gates down at the moment the largest moves begin. That belongs to the router section, not to a gate.</p>
<p><strong>The rule that would have been written, had the sign-flip control not killed it:</strong></p>
<blockquote><strong>ARM</strong> <code>vac_ign_brk</code> when <code>ATR(1m,14) ≥ 21.0pt</code> on MNQ <strong>and</strong> <code>13:00 ≤ t &lt; 21:00Z</code>. <strong>BENCH</strong> below <code>19.0pt</code> (2-point hysteresis, matching the existing 2-mark convention) with a <strong>≥5-minute dwell on the arm side</strong> — ATR crossing a bare threshold on a partial bar is exactly the oscillation that produced 20 switch changes for 4 trades on 08-07. <strong>No ER condition</strong>, for the reason above. Expectancy on that home regime only: <strong>+$18.03/trade over 95 trades</strong>. Blanket, as context and never as the verdict: <strong>+$3.21 over 284.</strong></blockquote>
<p><strong>Do not arm it.</strong> The home-regime number is the <em>same</em> number the fade control earns; conditioning on ATR-high selects trades with more range in both directions, and a gate that makes money whichever way you point it is a volatility harvester with a stop, not an edge.</p>
<p><strong>What we would need to measure to make this tradeable.</strong> A <strong>direction discriminator that is not price, not aggressor flow, and not top-3 book depth</strong> — all three are ruled out above at tight error bars. The concrete candidates, in order of how cheaply we could instrument them: (i) <strong>full-depth book imbalance beyond level 3</strong>, including queue-position and cancel/replace rate — <code>depth.db</code> carries the wide snapshot but at 250ms it cannot see the fleeting quotes <code>capture.db.book</code> does at 41ms, so this needs the 41ms feed widened past level 3, which is an IBKR subscription question, not a code question; (ii) <strong>cross-instrument lead-lag</strong> — we already capture MGC L1+L2 and MES/M2K depth, and none of this week's work looked at whether gold or the breadth complex turns first; (iii) <strong>a trade-size distribution split</strong> (large-lot vs small-lot aggressor), which the tick stream already carries in <code>size</code> and which no census or gate on this desk currently uses. (iii) is free and is the one I would run first next Friday.</p>
<p><strong>MGC: deliberately not attempted.</strong> The scope is explicit that gold gates are <strong>invented fresh, never ported or re-tuned from an MNQ gate</strong>, and the VACUUM cluster is a property of the MNQ census. Porting VAC-IGN-BRK to gold would have been the wrong kind of easy number — and at $10.00/point a mis-priced transfer would have looked five times more exciting than it was. Gold is <code>gf_MGC</code>'s work.</p>
