<h4>GREENFIELD — THE CHOP-DAY TURN SCALP (L2-LED)</h4>
<h5>The short version</h5>
<p>Garrath, you asked for a purpose-built chop-turn scalper: catch the oscillation turns on the days the desk currently donates, bank tiny, and <strong>lead with the order book</strong> — same lineage as <code>exhaustion_short</code>, which was built out of L2 footprints and is one of the best things we own.</p>
<p>I built it and raced it tick by tick on <strong>17 full-stack trading days</strong> — 20.7 million trade ticks, <strong>261.9 million 41-millisecond ten-deep book rows</strong>, 4.6 million 250ms depth snapshots and 26.2 million quote updates. Sixteen named candidates, 768 costed parameter cells in the main sweep and 1,080 more in the finalist search, every fill at $1.50 the round trip.</p>
<p><strong>The answer is no, and this time the cause of death is not the one I expected.</strong></p>
<p><strong>1. Your lead hypothesis is refuted, and it points the wrong way.</strong> Requiring the far side to be ABSORBING — a big resting wall on the side price has to eat to continue — does not help the fade. It <strong>hurts</strong> it, consistently, in two independent tests. Unfiltered chop turns drift <strong>+0.054 pt</strong> in the fade's favour over the next minute. Add "wall ≥ 1.5×" and that becomes <strong>−0.117 pt</strong>. Add "five-deep wall ≥ 1.3×" and it becomes <strong>−0.374 pt</strong> (t = −1.72). In the costed sweep the wall filter is the <strong>worst</strong> candidate on the board: <code>CT2_WALL</code> medians <strong>−$3.09/trade</strong> against the naive fade's −$2.54. The wall is not absorption at a range edge. It is a target.</p>
<p><strong>2. The one thing that did survive to the battery was killed by handing the null my own search.</strong> <code>CT15</code> — close at a 30-minute extreme, on a ≥4pt 20-second thrust, with the 41ms refill count above median — made <strong>+$158.00 on 26 trades, +$6.08 a trade, 77% win, positive in-sample AND out-of-sample, positive on 13 of 13 leave-one-day-out folds, still +$4.07/trade after stripping its best five</strong>. On a single pre-registered cell its shuffle p-value is <strong>0.017</strong>. But that cell was found by a 1,080-cell search, so I gave the <em>null</em> the same search: shuffle the entry features within each day and let it hunt. <strong>The null's own best cell averages +$8.42/trade and beats the real one 85% of the time.</strong> Matched fairly, at cells at least as wide as the real winner, <strong>p = 0.367</strong>. It is a search artefact.</p>
<p><strong>3. And even if it were real, the arithmetic does not reach your bar.</strong> <code>CT15</code> fires <strong>1.53 times a day</strong>. To clear +$250 on a chop day at that rate it must earn <strong>$163.50 a trade — 81.7 MNQ points</strong>. The median chop 30-minute block's <em>entire range</em> is <strong>51.75 points</strong>. Selectivity and the +$200–300 target are in direct conflict: at the measured $6.08/trade you need 41 trades a day, and the filter loose enough to fire 41 times a day is the naive fade at <strong>−$2.54</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>4. ★ The premise needs one correction, and it is the finding I would act on.</strong> The desk did not donate on this week's chop days. <strong>It did not trade them at all.</strong> On 2026-08-20 and 2026-08-21 the tournament took <strong>zero</strong> trades. The week's −$2,323 is <code>day_rider</code>'s book, not the gates'. Across the whole 18-day window the tournament's loss is concentrated in <strong>violent-whipsaw</strong> blocks (−$1,383.50 on 95 trades) — high ATR going nowhere — <strong>not</strong> in plain chop (−$403.00 on 58). That is a router bench to price, not a gate for greenfield to build.</p>
