<p>Three things fall out of that table, and they set up everything after it.</p>
<p><strong>The unfiltered chop turn earns 0.054 points against a 0.75-point cost.</strong> That is the known coin-flip, measured. It is not a cost problem you can fix with a cheaper broker; it is 7% of the fee.</p>
<p><strong>The wall has the wrong sign in both L2 sources.</strong> The quintile test agrees independently — on a symmetric 4pt/4pt first-touch race over 3,434 resolved chop turns (base 45.02%), the top quintile of the five-deep wall ratio reverts <strong>39.62%</strong> against the bottom quintile's <strong>45.93%</strong>, z = <strong>−2.37</strong>; the level-1 wall gives z = <strong>−2.03</strong>. Every other book term I built is inside noise:</p>
