<h5>9. ★ THE KILL — give the null the same search that found the survivor</h5>
<p><code>CT15</code> was not pre-registered. It is the winner of a search over <strong>6 thrust thresholds × 12 book terms × 15 exit cells = 1,080 costed cells</strong>, on a pool of 104 events. A 1.7% p-value on the best of 1,080 cells is not a 1.7% p-value on anything.</p>
<p>So I built the null tape and ran the <strong>entire search on it</strong>, 300 times.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Null B — SHUFFLE.</strong> Permute the entry features across events <em>within each day</em>. Every marginal distribution and each day's outcome mix is preserved exactly; only the feature→outcome link dies.</li><li><strong>Null A — DIRECTION.</strong> Flip each event's side at random, so the fade becomes a chase.</li></ul>
<table><tr><th></th><th>real</th><th>Null B (shuffle) best cell</th><th>Null A (direction) best cell</th></tr><tr><td>best $/trade found by the search</td><td class="num"><strong>+$6.077</strong></td><td>mean <strong>+$8.422</strong>, median +$8.396, p95 +$12.418</td><td>mean +$8.122, median +$8.375</td></tr><tr><td>median n of the winning cell</td><td class="num">26</td><td class="num">16</td><td class="num">14</td></tr><tr><td><strong>p(null ≥ real), unrestricted</strong></td><td>—</td><td class="num"><strong>0.853</strong></td><td class="num">0.790</td></tr><tr><td><strong>p(null ≥ real), n-matched (cells n ≥ 26)</strong></td><td>—</td><td class="num"><strong>0.367</strong></td><td class="num">0.150</td></tr></table>
<p><strong>The null, handed the same grid, finds a better cell than the real one 85% of the time.</strong> That comparison is unfair to the null in one direction — it wins by picking 14- and 16-trade corners where the variance is enormous — so I matched it: restrict the null to cells at least as wide as the real winner. It still lands at <strong>p = 0.367</strong>. There is no reading of this test on which <code>CT15</code> is significant.</p>
<p>This is the same failure mode last week's section found by a different route (a placebo beating the best cell 77% of the time). Two independent weeks, two independent harnesses, the same answer: <strong>the deep-extreme chop fade is search noise.</strong></p>
<hr class="frag-sep">
