<p><strong>VERDICT — the chop-day turn scalp DID NOT SURVIVE.</strong> The lead candidate <code>CT15</code> (chop tape, bar closes at a 30-minute extreme, ≥4pt 20-second thrust, 41ms refill count above median, 8pt/10pt) was killed by the <strong>placebo/shuffle test run with the search included</strong> — the null, handed the same 1,080-cell grid, finds a better cell than the real +$6.077/trade <strong>85.3% of the time</strong> unrestricted and <strong>36.7% of the time</strong> when fairly matched at n ≥ 26 — and, independently, by the <strong>all-chop-days requirement</strong>, on which it is <strong>−$7.50 per chop day and green on 1 of the 3 chop days it traded</strong>. It passed everything else: out-of-sample (+$79.00 = in-sample exactly), leave-one-day-out (13/13), strip-the-best-5 (+$4.07/trade). The operator's core L2 hypothesis — that at a price extreme the far side ABSORBING separates a turn from a break — is <strong>REFUTED with the sign inverted</strong>: a hit-side wall ≥ 1.5× turns the chop turn's +0.054pt one-minute drift into <strong>−0.117pt</strong> (five-deep ≥ 1.3× → <strong>−0.374pt, t = −1.72</strong>; quintile z = <strong>−2.37</strong>), and <code>CT2_WALL</code> is the <strong>worst</strong> of sixteen candidates at −$3.09/trade. The VWAP-flat filter is <strong>inert</strong> ($0.01/trade across 48 cells). The naive fade is a <strong>sign problem, not a cost problem</strong> — negative in 48 of 48 cells before a cent of fee. Projected chop-day P&amp;L is <strong>+$6.88 (CT13) to −$7.50 (CT15) per chop day against the +$200–300 bar</strong>, and the gap is structural: at 1.53 fires a day the rule must earn <strong>81.7 points a trade</strong> out of a chop block whose whole range is <strong>51.75</strong>. The section's transferable results are elsewhere — <strong>(1)</strong> the desk does not donate in chop, it donated <strong>−$1,383.50 on 95 trades in violent-whipsaw blocks</strong> (and took <strong>zero</strong> tournament trades on this week's chop days, 08-20 and 08-21), which is a two-term router bench worth ~10× this scalper; <strong>(2)</strong> the resting wall is a target, not absorption; <strong>(3)</strong> the only L2 term that ever helped is the <strong>41ms refill count</strong>, which the 250ms depth sample cannot see.</p>
