<h6>1.3 The $7.50 round trip is not the cost, and that is already fixed</h6>
<p>Last week's section led with <em>"a round trip in gold costs $7.50, not $1.50"</em> — 0.30pt of spread × 2 legs × $10, plus the fee. That figure double-counts. Crossing the spread costs you <strong>half</strong> the spread against the mid on each leg, so a round trip crosses it <strong>once</strong>, not twice: $3.00 of spread plus $1.50 of commission = <strong>$4.50 all-in from the mid.</strong></p>
<p>I measured this independently before finding it had already been caught: across the 288 fades in this section the mean gap between a mid-to-mid mark and the spread-honest race is <strong>$0.38 a trade</strong>, nothing like $6.00. The 08-15 audit had reached the same conclusion the same day and shipped the correction into <code>shadow_mgc.py</code> (<code>MGC_FEE_RT</code> $7.50 → $4.50). Worth knowing why it matters: that error is what made the coil bouncer read −$454 and therefore dead; at the true cost it is +$8.42, which is breakeven rather than a grave.</p>
<p>So the honest cost lever is not a fixed fee — the racer fills at real bid and ask already. It is <strong>slippage</strong>, and every candidate below is swept against it in ticks (MGC tick = 0.10pt = $1.00).</p>
