<p>And the fires themselves are only two-thirds the same event: <strong>129 depth-mid fires, 124 trade-bar fires, 86 in both</strong> — 67% of the lab's exist in production, 69% of production's exist in the lab.</p>
<p>That reconciles everything. The trade-bar column has the same sign and roughly the same magnitude as what the live shadow actually recorded (control −$7.29 in replay vs −$14.24 live; hole arms −$8.96 and −$6.55 vs −$14.64 and −$21.93 — live worse, but n=7 a side). <strong>There is no mystery and no bug. The lab and the desk are running different gates, and the desk is running the worse one.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Why the mid is the better input, in plain English.</strong> A "sixty-minute high" taken from trades is the highest price at which somebody happened to <em>transact</em>; taken from the depth mid it is the highest price the market was <em>showing</em>. Gold's book updates four times a second whether or not anyone trades, so its extremes are a cleaner statement of where the level actually was. The gate is a statement about a <strong>level</strong>, and the mid knows where the level is.</p>
<p>⚠ <strong>I have to correct my own first draft on the mechanism here.</strong> I initially wrote that the trade tape is <em>sparser</em> — fewer minutes, so staler extremes. On the sixteen days both of my tapes cover, that is simply false: <strong>21,699 depth-mid minutes against 21,600 trade-bar minutes, a dead heat.</strong> The two tapes do not differ in how many minutes they have; they differ in <strong>where the high and the low of each minute sit</strong>, and that is enough to move a third of the fires. Note also that my trade-bar proxy is the 5-second lake bars resampled, which is close to but not identical to what <code>md</code> actually folds — the 08-15 audit measured a much larger minute gap (29,565 vs 13,440) against production's real bar stream. So the <em>direction</em> of this finding is solid and triple-confirmed, but the exact mechanism by which the two tapes diverge is not fully pinned down, and I am not going to pretend otherwise.</p>
<p><strong>⚠ And the caveat that stops me claiming more than that.</strong> The lab's forward result leans hard on one day. <code>fade_primary</code> made +$512 across the seven forward days, of which <strong>+$341 is 2026-08-25 alone</strong> — and 08-25 has no trade-bar file, so it is the one day this comparison cannot check. Strip it and the lab's six checkable days are +$171 against production's −$475. The direction of the finding is unchanged, but "the mid tape wins" currently rests on six days and a fire-overlap argument, not on a fortress.</p>
<p><strong>The recommendation, and it is a build not a study:</strong> point the MGC shadow's bar builder at the depth mid, keep the existing trade-bar arms running alongside as the control, and let the two accumulate side by side. The service already opens <code>DepthFeed("MGC")</code> and <code>book_at()</code> already returns <code>bid1p</code>/<code>ask1p</code>, so the mid is sitting in a variable the process already holds. <strong>That is the single highest-value change on the gold line and nothing else here should be acted on before it.</strong></p>
