<h5>6. ★★ THE UNTURNED STONE — gold against the Nasdaq</h5>
<p>Last week's §13 named exactly one thing never tried: <em>"the relationship between MGC and MNQ on the same clock … a break in gold that coincides with a matching break in the Nasdaq is a different event from one that does not, and nothing in this section can tell them apart."</em></p>
<p>We hold 278 paired trading days of both symbols' minute bars. Everything is strictly causal — MNQ's state is read at the gold signal bar, and MNQ's own 60-minute extreme is shifted so the signal bar is excluded. Every cut is placebo-controlled by <strong>keeping the same number of trades at random, 200 times</strong>, because a filter that keeps 10% of trades looks brilliant whenever the 90% it dropped lost.</p>
<h6>6.1 It works, and it is a veto rather than a confirm</h6>
<table><tr><th>FADE the gold break, 278 paired days</th><th>n</th><th>net</th><th>$/trade</th><th>filter placebo</th></tr><tr><td>blanket (control)</td><td class="num">3,800</td><td class="num">+$6,522</td><td class="num">+$1.72</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MNQ is CONFIRMING the break</strong> (breaking the same way)</td><td class="num">389</td><td class="num"><strong>−$4,093</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>−$10.52</strong></td><td><strong>beaten 195/200 — 2.5th pctile</strong></td></tr><tr><td>MNQ's 15-min drift AGREES with the break</td><td class="num">2,417</td><td class="num">−$543</td><td class="num">−$0.22</td><td>beaten 188/200 — 6.0th pctile</td></tr><tr><td>MNQ is not breaking at all</td><td class="num">3,324</td><td class="num">+$8,926</td><td class="num">+$2.69</td><td>beaten 18/200 — 91.0th pctile</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MNQ's drift OPPOSES the break</strong></td><td class="num">1,345</td><td class="num"><strong>+$7,299</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>+$5.43</strong></td><td><strong>beaten 6/200 — 97.0th pctile</strong></td></tr></table>
<p>This is the strongest form the test can take: <strong>both sides move, in opposite directions, well past random.</strong> The bad cut is significantly <em>worse</em> than a random keep and the good cut significantly better. Fading a gold break the Nasdaq agrees with turns a +$1.72 blanket into −$10.52; fading one the Nasdaq is leaning against turns it into +$5.43, more than tripling the edge on 35% of the trades.</p>
<p><strong>The mechanism is plausible and was predicted before pricing:</strong> a gold break that coincides with a Nasdaq break is a macro risk event with real flow behind it, so it <em>keeps going</em>. A gold break with a quiet Nasdaq is gold's own idiosyncratic noise, and noise snaps back.</p>
<h6>6.2 ⚠ …and it does not show up on the 29 days where the book gate lives</h6>
<table><tr><th><code>fade_primary</code>, 29 days</th><th>n</th><th>net</th><th>$/trade</th><th></th><th>forward 7d</th></tr><tr><td>no veto</td><td class="num">288</td><td class="num">+$3,243</td><td class="num">+$11.26</td><td></td><td>+$512 (+$7.43)</td></tr><tr><td>veto when MNQ confirms</td><td class="num">244</td><td class="num">+$2,566</td><td class="num">+$10.52</td><td></td><td>+$445 (+$7.29)</td></tr><tr><td>veto when MNQ's drift agrees</td><td class="num">95</td><td class="num">+$851</td><td class="num">+$8.96</td><td></td><td><strong>+$336 (+$12.46)</strong></td></tr><tr><td>filter placebo of the confirm-veto</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td><strong>beaten 132/200 — 34th pctile</strong></td></tr></table>
<p>On 288 trades the veto is indistinguishable from random and reduces the total. On 3,800 it is a strong two-sided result. The year sample is thirteen times larger and the book sample has 44 trades in the vetoed bucket, so I lean towards the year — but I am not going to fold an unconfirmed cut into the primary arm on that reasoning.</p>
<p><strong>Disposition: a SEPARATE shadow arm.</strong> <code>mgc_holebreak_fade_{long,short}_xveto</code> alongside the existing pair, identical in every other respect. That is the only way to attribute it, it costs nothing, and in a few weeks the book will simply say which sample was right.</p>
<h6>6.3 The cross-asset momentum attack — a null, and a clean one</h6>
<p>Rule 2 says hunt momentum deliberately. Every previous gold momentum gate has been built on gold's own tape, and that family is refuted six ways. A different instrument as the confirm is genuinely new. It does not work:</p>
