<h5>7. THE VOLATILITY CELL — right verdict last week, wrong reason, and a look-ahead of my own</h5>
<p>Rule 2 asks explicitly: if direction is unpredictable but <em>size</em> is predictable, does a breakout-either-way beat a directional gate? Last week refuted it on 22 days — the tightest 20-minute range quintile went on to move <em>less</em> than average. That deserves the 320-day tape as much as any survivor does, and it turns out the reasoning was wrong even though the verdict was right.</p>
<p><strong>The two measures disagree completely, on identical rows, 296,475 minutes:</strong></p>
<table><tr><th>sorted on the RAW 20-min range (last week's measure)</th><th>n</th><th>next-60min move, $</th><th>vs all</th><th>in ATR units</th><th>vs all</th></tr><tr><td>Q1 tightest</td><td class="num">59,295</td><td class="num">$49.05</td><td class="num">−$50.92</td><td class="num">10.60</td><td class="num"><strong>+4.75</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Q5 widest</td><td class="num">59,295</td><td class="num">$186.33</td><td class="num">+$86.36</td><td class="num">3.83</td><td class="num">−2.02</td></tr></table>
<table><tr><th>sorted on 20-min range <strong>÷ ATR</strong> (relative quiet)</th><th>n</th><th>next-60min move, $</th><th>vs all</th><th>in ATR units</th><th>vs all</th></tr><tr><td>Q1 tightest</td><td class="num">59,295</td><td class="num">$110.52</td><td class="num"><strong>+$10.56</strong></td><td class="num">4.62</td><td class="num">−1.23</td></tr><tr><td>Q5 widest</td><td class="num">59,295</td><td class="num">$74.15</td><td class="num"><strong>−$25.81</strong></td><td class="num">10.48</td><td class="num">+4.63</td></tr></table>
<p>Rank correlation with the next 60 minutes' size: raw range <strong>+0.371</strong>, range÷ATR <strong>−0.134</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Why they disagree.</strong> Rank corr(ATR, next-60-min size) is <strong>+0.412</strong> — big begets big. So sorting on the raw range mostly sorts on the <em>volatility level</em>, and last week's table was measuring volatility clustering, not compression. Dividing by ATR asks the straddle's actual question — <em>is this quiet relative to its own recent behaviour</em> — and the sign flips: relatively quiet gold does go on to move <strong>more</strong> in dollars. <strong>Last week's stated reason for the refutation was an artefact.</strong></p>
<p><strong>So I traded it</strong>, because a gradient in dollars can still be worth nothing once the stop scales to the same small ATR. Either-way entry: stop orders at ±k·ATR around a compressed minute, 60-minute arming window, take whichever fills, no direction called anywhere.</p>
