<p><strong>All eight compression cells negative; the control negative too.</strong> The best cell is −$0.32 a trade, 4 green months of 13, strip-best-3 takes it to −$8,591, drop-best-day −$6,258, and one tick of extra slippage per leg takes it to −$2.32. <strong>REFUTED again — this time on 320 days and by an actual trade rather than a gradient, and for the correct reason:</strong> compressed gold does expand more in dollars, but <em>less</em> in ATR units, and once the stop is scaled to the same ATR there is nothing left.</p>
<h6>7.1 ⚠ The look-ahead I put in it, and how it read before I found it</h6>
<p>The first version of that harness reported the control at <strong>+$71,608, +$14.25 a trade, 62% win.</strong> That number is a bug and it is worth showing you exactly which one, because it is a class this desk keeps meeting.</p>
<p>The scan found the bar whose <em>high</em> or <em>low</em> breached the trigger level, then entered at <strong>that same bar's open</strong>. But a bar's extremes are only known once it has closed — so it was buying at a price it had only chosen <em>because it had already seen the rest of that bar</em>. A guaranteed favourable fill on every single trade. Entering at the open of the <em>next</em> bar, which is the first price genuinely available after the signal, turned <strong>+$71,608 into −$12,462.</strong></p>
<p>It is the same family as the <code>resample</code> left-edge trap that cost the coil gate +$906 → +$470 on 08-14, and it produced by far the biggest number in this report. Anything in a gold study that looks like $14 a trade over five thousand trades is a bug until proven otherwise.</p>
