<h5>15. THE ONE STONE STILL UNTURNED</h5>
<p>Last week's stone — cross-asset — is turned, and it paid (§6). The next one is narrower and it is the direct consequence of §3.</p>
<p><strong>We have never checked whether gold's <em>book</em> is as sparse as gold's <em>trades</em>.</strong> The whole bar-source finding says the depth mid is a better price series than the trade tape because gold prints sporadically. But the liquidity filter reads that same book, and if the book is <em>also</em> thin or stale in exactly the minutes where trades are absent, then the "empty far side" the gate selects for may sometimes be <strong>an absent feed rather than an absent bid</strong>. Last week checked one version of this — 72% of hole rows had size in front 60 seconds earlier — but that was on the depth-mid tape and it did not ask whether the <em>quiet-tape</em> holes and the <em>busy-tape</em> holes pay differently.</p>
<p><strong>The concrete next three runs, in order:</strong></p>
<ol><li><strong>Build the depth-mid bar source into the MGC shadow</strong> (§12.1) and let both arms run. Nothing else on this line is worth doing first.</li><li><strong>Split the hole cell by tape activity</strong> — trades-per-minute in the minute before the break — and check the empty-book cell still pays in the busiest quartile. That is the cheapest possible test of the concern above.</li><li><strong>Re-run <code>mgc_coil_bounce</code> at the corrected $4.50 cost</strong> on the 29-day quote tape. It was killed at $7.50 and the audit already says it is breakeven at the true cost; it deserves the honest look it did not get.</li></ol>
